QB.add(4,[{"id":4001,"t":"Periods of Development","f":"em6","s":"A conceptus has just implanted in the uterine wall and is 12 days old. Which period of development is it in?","o":["Pre-embryonic","Embryonic","Foetal","Neonatal","Perinatal"],"e":"The <b>pre-embryonic period</b> runs from <b>conception to week 2</b> and includes fertilisation, formation of the morula and blastocyst, and <b>implantation</b> (days 6–13).","w":["Weeks 3–8.","Week 9 to birth.","Not a period in this lecture.","Not a period in this lecture."],"k":"Pre-embryonic: conception–week 2 (includes implantation).","rn":[["","When is the pre-embryonic period?","When is the pre-embryonic period?","Conception to end of week 2"],["","What are the 3 periods of development?","What are the 3 periods of development?","Pre-embryonic; Embryonic; Foetal"],["","What happens in the pre-embryonic period?","What happens in the pre-embryonic period?","Fertilisation; Morula and blastocyst form; Blastocyst implants; Bilaminar disc forms"]]},{"id":4002,"t":"Periods of Development","f":"em6","s":"During which period do the germ layers develop, the placenta start to form and the key body systems form from the trilaminar disc?","o":["Embryonic period (weeks 3–8)","Pre-embryonic period (conception–week 2)","Foetal period (week 9–birth)","Week 38 onwards","Weeks 1–2 only"],"e":"The <b>embryonic period (weeks 3–8)</b>: development of the <b>germ layers</b>, the <b>placenta</b> starts to develop, and key <b>body systems</b> form from the trilaminar disc.","w":["Fertilisation to implantation.","Final maturation.","After birth.","Pre-embryonic."],"k":"Embryonic: weeks 3–8, germ layers and systems.","rn":[["","What happens in the embryonic period?","What happens in the embryonic period?","Germ layers develop; Placenta starts to develop; Body systems form from the trilaminar disc"],["","When is the embryonic period?","When is the embryonic period?","Week 3 to end of week 8"]]},{"id":4003,"t":"Periods of Development","s":"In the foetal period, which statement describes the main developmental change?","o":["Final maturation: viscera grow, musculoskeletal system becomes functional","Germ-layer formation: the bilaminar disc becomes the trilaminar disc","Organ formation: the body systems are first laid down from the germ layers","Folding: the flat disc folds longitudinally and laterally into a body shape","Implantation: the conceptus embeds and remodels the maternal blood supply"],"e":"The <b>foetal period (week 9 – birth at week 38)</b> is <b>final maturation</b>: continued growth of the viscera and body systems, and the <b>musculoskeletal system becomes fully functional</b>.","w":["That is gastrulation, week 3.","Body systems form in the embryonic period (weeks 3–8).","Folding is week 4 (embryonic period).","Implantation is days 6–13 (pre-embryonic)."],"k":"Foetal period = maturation and growth.","rn":[["","What happens in the foetal period?","What happens in the foetal period?","Final maturation; Growth of the viscera and body systems; Musculoskeletal system becomes fully functional"]]},{"id":4004,"t":"Periods of Development","s":"Pregnancy is usually said to last 40 weeks. Why does the developmental timeline end at week 38?","o":["Full term in developmental terms is classified at 38 weeks","Birth always happens at exactly 38 weeks of development","Weeks 39–40 are counted as part of the neonatal period","Weeks 39–40 are counted as part of the embryonic period","The foetal period ends at 36 weeks, followed by maturation"],"e":"The lecturer explained that pregnancy is recognised as 40 weeks, but <b>full term in terms of development</b> is classified at the <b>38-week</b> point.","w":["Birth timing varies; 38 weeks is the developmental full term.","Not the explanation given.","The embryonic period ends at week 8.","The foetal period runs to week 38."],"k":"Developmental full term = 38 weeks.","rn":[["","Why is full term 38 weeks, not 40?","Why is full term 38 weeks, not 40?","Pregnancy is recognised as 40 weeks; Full term in terms of development is classified at 38 weeks"]]},{"id":4005,"t":"Periods of Development","s":"An embryo at day 50 is examined. Which statement is correct?","o":["It is still in the embryonic period, which ends at week 8","It is in the foetal period, which begins at week 6","It is in the pre-embryonic period, which ends at week 8","It is in the foetal period, which begins at day 42","It is in the embryonic period, which ends at week 12"],"e":"Day 50 is about week 7–8. The <b>embryonic period runs to week 8</b>; the foetal period begins at <b>week 9</b>. (Memory aid “2 – 8 – 38”.)","w":["The foetal period starts at week 9.","The pre-embryonic period ends at week 2.","The foetal period starts at week 9.","The embryonic period ends at week 8 (2 – 8 – 38)."],"k":"“2 – 8 – 38”: period boundaries.","rn":[["","When is the embryonic period?","When is the embryonic period?","Week 3 to end of week 8"],["","When is the foetal period?","When is the foetal period?","Week 9 to birth (week 38)"],["","When is the pre-embryonic period?","When is the pre-embryonic period?","Conception to end of week 2"]]},{"id":4006,"t":"Germ Layers and Their Derivatives","s":"Name the subdivision of mesoderm labelled <b>A</b>, lying immediately next to the neural tube.","e":"A is paraxial mesoderm, the cells immediately lateral to the neural tube and notochord. It divides into somites and forms skeletal muscle, the skeleton, dermis and connective tissue.","k":"A = paraxial mesoderm.","img":{"src":"4/mesoderm21","cap":"Transverse section of an embryo at 21 days. Labels removed."},"a":["paraxial mesoderm","paraxial"],"rn":[["","What does Paraxial Mesoderm form?","What does Paraxial Mesoderm form?","Skeletal muscle; Majority of the skeleton; Dermis; Connective tissue"],["","Paraxial Mesoderm","Paraxial Mesoderm","Immediately next to the axis; Flanks the notochord / neural tube; Increase in number of cells lateral to the neural tube"]]},{"id":4007,"t":"Periods of Development","f":"em7","s":"Trace the normal route in week 1. Where does the oocyte come from, where is it normally fertilised, and where does the blastocyst implant?","o":["Ovary → uterine tube → uterus","Ovary → uterus → uterine tube","Uterine tube → ovary → uterus","Ovary → uterine tube → vaginal canal","Uterus → uterine tube → ovary"],"e":"The oocyte is <b>ovulated from the ovary</b>, <b>fertilised in the uterine tube</b>, and the conceptus travels to the <b>uterus</b> for implantation.","w":["Fertilisation is in the tube, then implantation in the uterus.","The oocyte comes from the ovary.","Implantation is in the uterus.","The oocyte starts in the ovary."],"k":"Ovary → uterine tube (fertilisation) → uterus (implantation).","rn":[["","What is the most common site of fertilisation?","What is the most common site of fertilisation?","Ampulla of the uterine (fallopian) tube"]]},{"id":4008,"t":"Week 1 of Development​","s":"How long after ovulation does fertilisation occur?","o":["12–24 hours","2–4 hours","3 days","4–5 days","6–13 days"],"e":"Fertilisation happens <b>12–24 hours after ovulation</b> (week 1, day 0).","w":["Too early.","Morula forms ~day 3.","Blastocyst.","Implantation."],"k":"Fertilisation: 12–24 h after ovulation.","rn":[["","When does fertilisation occur?","When does fertilisation occur?","12-24 hours after ovulation"]]},{"id":4009,"t":"Week 1 of Development​","s":"Name the layer around the oocyte that a spermatozoon must pass through, acting as a barrier to fertilisation.","e":"The successful spermatozoon must pass the corona radiata and zona pellucida, which acts as a barrier.","k":"Zona pellucida.","a":["zona pellucida","zona"],"rn":[["","Which layers must the spermatozoon pass through?","Which layers must the spermatozoon pass through?","Corona radiata; Zona pellucida"],["","What does the zona pellucida act as?","What does the zona pellucida act as?","A barrier"]]},{"id":4010,"t":"Week 1 of Development​","s":"After the spermatozoon has entered the oocyte, which pronucleus forms first?","o":["The female pronucleus, then the male","The male pronucleus, then the female","Both form at exactly the same time","The female pronucleus only; the male fuses directly","The male pronucleus only; the female fuses directly"],"e":"The lecturer stated the <b>female pronucleus forms first, then the male pronucleus</b>, both inside the ovum. When they join, a diploid zygote results.","w":["The lecturer said female first.","The female forms first.","Two pronuclei form.","Two pronuclei form."],"k":"Female pronucleus first, then male.","rn":[["","Which pronucleus forms first?","Which pronucleus forms first?","Female"],["","What are the steps of fertilisation, in order?","What are the steps of fertilisation, in order?","1. Two germ cells (half the genetic material each); 2. Spermatozoon enters: ovum; 3. Female then male pronucleus form; 4. Pronuclei join: zygote (complete set)"]]},{"id":4011,"t":"Week 1 of Development​","s":"Name the first cell to contain a complete diploid set of 46 chromosomes.","e":"When the female and male pronuclei join, the result is a single diploid cell with 46 chromosomes: the zygote.","k":"Zygote.","a":["zygote"],"rn":[["","Zygote","Zygote","Formed when the 2 pronuclei join; Diploid: 46 chromosomes"],["","What happens when the two pronuclei join?","What happens when the two pronuclei join?","A single nucleus forms; With a complete set of chromosomes; Diploid cell; Called the zygote"]]},{"id":4012,"t":"Week 1 of Development​","s":"After the spermatozoon has entered the oocyte but before the pronuclei join, what is the fertilised cell called?","e":"Once the spermatozoon is inside, the fertilised cell is called an ovum; the female then male pronuclei form, and when they join the cell becomes the zygote.","k":"Ovum.","a":["ovum"],"rn":[["","What is the oocyte called once the sperm has entered?","What is the oocyte called once the sperm has entered?","Ovum (fertilised ovum)"],["","What are the steps of fertilisation, in order?","What are the steps of fertilisation, in order?","1. Two germ cells (half the genetic material each); 2. Spermatozoon enters: ovum; 3. Female then male pronucleus form; 4. Pronuclei join: zygote (complete set)"]]},{"id":4013,"t":"Week 1 of Development​","s":"Which sequence correctly summarises fertilisation?","o":["Two haploid germ cells → ovum → two pronuclei → zygote with a full set","Two diploid germ cells → ovum → two pronuclei → zygote with 92 chromosomes","Two haploid germ cells → zygote → two pronuclei → ovum with a full set","Two haploid germ cells → ovum → one pronucleus → zygote with half a set","Two haploid germ cells → morula → two pronuclei → zygote with a full set"],"e":"In short: <b>two germ cells</b>, each with <b>half</b> the genetic material → <b>ovum</b> → <b>two pronuclei</b> → <b>zygote</b> with the complete set (46).","w":["Germ cells each carry half the genetic material.","The pronuclei form before the zygote.","Two pronuclei join to give a complete set.","The morula comes after the zygote."],"k":"Half + half → zygote with full set.","rn":[["","What are the steps of fertilisation, in order?","What are the steps of fertilisation, in order?","1. Two germ cells (half the genetic material each); 2. Spermatozoon enters: ovum; 3. Female then male pronucleus form; 4. Pronuclei join: zygote (complete set)"]]},{"id":4014,"t":"Week 1 of Development​","f":"em9","s":"The zygote divides repeatedly into smaller cells while the overall size stays about the same. What is this process, what type of division is it, and what are the cells called?","o":["Cleavage; mitosis; blastomeres","Gastrulation; meiosis; blastomeres","Cleavage; meiosis; trophoblasts","Neurulation; mitosis; epiblasts","Implantation; mitosis; embryoblasts"],"e":"The zygote undergoes <b>cleavage</b>: repeated <b>mitotic</b> divisions producing identical daughter cells called <b>blastomeres</b>. The cells get smaller while overall size stays about the same.","w":["Gastrulation forms the trilaminar disc; cleavage uses mitosis.","Mitosis, and the cells are blastomeres.","Neurulation forms the neural tube.","Implantation is days 6–13."],"k":"Cleavage = mitosis → blastomeres.","rn":[["","Cleavage","Cleavage","Repeated mitotic divisions of the zygote; Makes cells called blastomeres"],["","What type of cell division is cleavage?","What type of cell division is cleavage?","Mitosis"]]},{"id":4015,"t":"Week 1 of Development​","s":"Name the solid ball of 12–16 blastomeres present about 3 days after fertilisation.","e":"About day 3 the conceptus is a morula (12–16 cells, no cavity); by days 4–5 cavities merge into a blastocoele, forming the blastocyst.","k":"Morula.","a":["morula"],"rn":[["","How does the blastocoele form?","How does the blastocoele form?","Cavities develop in the morula; Cavities merge"],["","Morula","Morula","16 cell stage embryo; Solid ball of blastomeres"],["","Morula: how many cells does it have?","Morula: how many cells does it have?","12-16 cells"]]},{"id":4016,"t":"Week 1 of Development​","s":"How does a morula become a blastocyst?","o":["Cavities appear in the cell mass and merge into one blastocoele","The cells fuse into one large cell with a central cavity","The zona pellucida splits to create a central cavity","The cells stop dividing and fluid enters between them","The inner cell mass hollows out to form the amniotic cavity"],"e":"Cell division continues; <b>cavities appear</b> within the cell mass and <b>unite into one large cavity</b> (the blastocoele). The structure is then a <b>blastocyst</b>.","w":["Cell division continues; cells stay separate.","Not described.","Division continues.","The amniotic cavity forms later in week 2."],"k":"Blastocyst = morula + merged cavities (blastocoele).","rn":[["","How does the blastocoele form?","How does the blastocoele form?","Cavities develop in the morula; Cavities merge"]]},{"id":4017,"t":"Week 1 of Development​","s":"Name the outer single-cell layer of the blastocyst that invades the uterus and forms the foetal part of the placenta.","e":"The trophoblast (outer layer) invades the uterus and becomes the foetal component of the placenta; the embryoblast (inner cell mass) becomes the embryo.","k":"Trophoblast.","a":["trophoblast"],"rn":[["","Trophoblast","Trophoblast","Outer cell layer of the blastocyst; A single layer of cells; Becomes the foetal part of the placenta"]]},{"id":4018,"t":"Week 1 of Development​","f":"em11","s":"In a blastocyst micrograph, a clump of cells sits on one side, inside a thin ring of cells around a large empty space. Which labels are correct?","o":["Clump = embryoblast; ring = trophoblast; space = blastocoele","Clump = trophoblast; ring = embryoblast; space = blastocoele","Clump = embryoblast; ring = trophoblast; space = amniotic cavity","Clump = epiblast; ring = hypoblast; space = yolk sac","Clump = embryoblast; ring = cytotrophoblast; space = chorionic cavity"],"e":"The blastocyst has three parts: <b>embryoblast</b> (inner cell mass, the clump), <b>trophoblast</b> (outer single-cell ring) and <b>blastocoele</b> (fluid-filled cavity).","w":["Reversed.","The cavity is the blastocoele.","Epiblast/hypoblast form later (bilaminar disc).","The trophoblast splits later; the space is the blastocoele."],"k":"Blastocyst = embryoblast + trophoblast + blastocoele.","rn":[["","What are the 3 parts of the blastocyst?","What are the 3 parts of the blastocyst?","Blastocoele; Embryoblast; Trophoblast"]]},{"id":4019,"t":"Week 1 of Development​","s":"When is the blastocyst fully formed?","o":["Days 4–5","Day 3","Day 1","Days 6–13","Days 8–10"],"e":"The blastocyst is <b>fully formed at 4–5 days</b>.","w":["Morula.","Fertilisation/early cleavage.","Implantation.","Bilaminar disc."],"k":"Blastocyst: days 4–5.","rn":[["","When is the blastocyst fully formed?","When is the blastocyst fully formed?","Days 4-5"]]},{"id":4020,"t":"Week 1 of Development​","s":"Which part of the blastocyst goes on to form the bilaminar disc and then the embryo?","o":["Embryoblast","Trophoblast","Blastocoele","Zona pellucida","Syncytiotrophoblast"],"e":"The <b>embryoblast</b> (inner cell mass) becomes the <b>embryo</b>; next it differentiates into the bilaminar disc (epiblast and hypoblast).","w":["Placenta.","Cavity.","Barrier around the oocyte.","Part of the trophoblast."],"k":"Embryoblast → bilaminar disc → embryo.","rn":[["","Embryoblast","Embryoblast","Inner cell mass of the blastocyst; Becomes the embryo"],["","Inner Cell Mass","Inner Cell Mass","Embryoblast"]]},{"id":4021,"t":"Week 1 of Development​","s":"Why are terms such as ventral, dorsal, cranial and caudal used for the embryo instead of anterior, posterior, superior and inferior?","o":["The embryo is not yet in the anatomical position","The embryo has no front, back, head or tail yet","The adult terms are used only after birth","The adult terms are used only in imaging","The embryo is curled, so the adult terms reverse"],"e":"The embryo is <b>not yet in the anatomical position</b>, so different terms are used: ventral (front), dorsal (back), cranial/cephalic (head), caudal (tail). Once a baby can be put into the anatomical position, the usual terms are used.","w":["It does have these; the terms differ.","They are used once in the anatomical position.","Not the reason.","Not the reason."],"k":"Embryo: ventral/dorsal/cranial/caudal (not in anatomical position).","rn":[["","Why are ventral, dorsal, cranial and caudal used in the embryo?","Why are ventral, dorsal, cranial and caudal used in the embryo?","Embryo is not yet in anatomical position; Structures and limbs are still developing"]]},{"id":4022,"t":"Week 1 of Development​","s":"What becomes of the small tail of the embryo?","o":["It regresses and becomes the coccyx","It regresses and becomes the umbilical cord","It persists and becomes the sacrum","It regresses and becomes the hindgut","It persists and forms the primitive streak"],"e":"The embryo has a small <b>tail</b> that <b>regresses and becomes the coccyx</b>.","w":["The cord comes from the connecting stalk.","It becomes the coccyx.","The hindgut forms from folding.","The streak appears on day 15."],"k":"Embryonic tail → coccyx.","rn":[["","What happens to the embryo's tail?","What happens to the embryo's tail?","It regresses; Becomes the coccyx"]]},{"id":4023,"t":"Week 1 of Development​","s":"An embryologist describes a structure as “caudal”. What is the adult equivalent term?","o":["Inferior","Superior","Anterior","Posterior","Medial"],"e":"<b>Caudal</b> (towards the tail end) ≈ <b>inferior</b>. Ventral ≈ anterior, dorsal ≈ posterior, cranial/cephalic ≈ superior.","w":["Cranial.","Ventral.","Dorsal.","Not equivalent."],"k":"Caudal ≈ inferior; cranial ≈ superior.","rn":[["","Caudal","Caudal","Towards the tail end; Most inferior"]]},{"id":4024,"t":"Part 2 - Week 2: Implantation and the Bilaminar Disc","s":"Between which days does implantation occur, and what does it involve?","o":["Days 6–13; the blastocyst invades the epithelium and endometrium","Days 6–13; the morula invades the epithelium and endometrium","Days 4–5; the blastocyst invades the epithelium and endometrium","Days 6–13; the blastocyst attaches to the uterine tube","Days 15–20; the trilaminar disc invades the endometrium"],"e":"<b>Implantation (days 6–13)</b>: the blastocyst <b>invades the uterine epithelium and endometrium</b>, the trophoblast differentiates and the inner cell mass differentiates further.","w":["It is the blastocyst that implants.","Days 4–5 is blastocyst formation.","Implantation is in the uterus.","Days 15–20 is gastrulation."],"k":"Implantation: days 6–13 into endometrium.","rn":[["","What does the blastocyst invade during implantation?","What does the blastocyst invade during implantation?","Uterine epithelium; Endometrium"],["","How deep does the implanting blastocyst go?","How deep does the implanting blastocyst go?","Through epithelium and endometrium; Up to a third of the myometrium (muscle layer)"]]},{"id":4025,"t":"Part 2 - Week 2: Implantation and the Bilaminar Disc","s":"During implantation, name the layer of trophoblast on the maternal side that keeps invading the uterine wall.","e":"The trophoblast splits into the syncytiotrophoblast (maternal side, invades) and the cytotrophoblast (single-cell layer on the embryo side).","k":"Syncytiotrophoblast.","a":["syncytiotrophoblast","syncytio-trophoblast","syncytiotrophoblast layer"],"rn":[["","Syncytiotrophoblast","Syncytiotrophoblast","Maternal side; Keeps invading further into the uterine wall"]]},{"id":4026,"t":"Part 2 - Week 2: Implantation and the Bilaminar Disc","s":"How deeply does the blastocyst usually embed in the uterine wall?","o":["Up to about a third of the myometrium","Up to about two-thirds of the myometrium","Into the epithelium only","Through the whole myometrium","Into the endometrium only"],"e":"The blastocyst embeds deep in the uterine wall, usually up to about <b>a third of the myometrium</b> (the middle muscular layer).","w":["About a third.","It embeds deep in the wall.","Not through the whole wall.","It reaches into the myometrium."],"k":"Implantation depth: ~⅓ of the myometrium.","rn":[["","How deep does the implanting blastocyst go?","How deep does the implanting blastocyst go?","Through epithelium and endometrium; Up to a third of the myometrium (muscle layer)"]]},{"id":4027,"t":"Part 2 - Week 2: Implantation and the Bilaminar Disc","s":"How does the implanting embryo obtain nutrient- and oxygen-rich blood?","o":["It remodels maternal uterine artery branches","It forms a new uterine artery of its own","It draws blood through the zona pellucida","It relies only on the amniotic fluid","It uses the blastocoele as a vessel"],"e":"The embryo <b>remodels the existing maternal blood supply</b> (from branches of the <b>uterine artery</b>) to bring nutrient- and oxygen-rich blood to itself: <b>vascular remodelling</b>.","w":["It remodels existing vessels.","Not described.","Not described.","Not described."],"k":"Vascular remodelling of maternal supply.","rn":[["","How does the embryo get its blood supply?","How does the embryo get its blood supply?","Remodels the existing maternal blood supply"]]},{"id":4028,"t":"Part 2 - Week 2: Implantation and the Bilaminar Disc","s":"In week 2 (days 8–10), the inner cell mass differentiates into which two layers?","o":["Epiblast and hypoblast","Ectoderm and endoderm","Syncytiotrophoblast and cytotrophoblast","Mesoderm and endoderm","Amnion and chorion"],"e":"The <b>inner cell mass (embryoblast)</b> differentiates into the <b>bilaminar disc</b>: <b>epiblast</b> (upper) and <b>hypoblast</b> (lower).","w":["Trilaminar disc layers (week 3).","Trophoblast derivatives.","Trilaminar.","Not the disc layers."],"k":"Bilaminar disc = epiblast + hypoblast.","rn":[["","What are the 2 layers of the bilaminar disc?","What are the 2 layers of the bilaminar disc?","Epiblast; Hypoblast"]]},{"id":4029,"t":"Part 2 - Week 2: Implantation and the Bilaminar Disc","f":"em15","s":"Which cavity forms in relation to the epiblast, and which membrane lines it?","o":["Amniotic cavity; amnion","Primary yolk sac; exocoelomic membrane","Chorionic cavity; amnion","Blastocoele; trophoblast","Amniotic cavity; exocoelomic membrane"],"e":"<b>Epiblast</b> → cavity = <b>amniotic cavity</b> (containing amniotic fluid), membrane = <b>amnion</b>. <b>Hypoblast</b> → cavity = <b>primary yolk sac</b>, membrane = <b>exocoelomic membrane</b>.","w":["Hypoblast.","Forms from merged coelom.","Blastocyst.","Wrong membrane."],"k":"Epiblast: amniotic cavity + amnion.","rn":[["","What is the amnion?","What is the amnion?","Membrane lining the amniotic cavity"],["","Epiblast","Epiblast","Upper layer of the bilaminar disc; Forms the amnion (maternal side); Its cavity is the amniotic cavity"]]},{"id":4030,"t":"Part 2 - Week 2: Implantation and the Bilaminar Disc","s":"What is the function of the primary yolk sac?","o":["Nutrition of the embryo","Producing amniotic fluid","Forming the placenta","Forming the notochord","Releasing soluble mediators for cell migration"],"e":"The <b>primary yolk sac</b> (hypoblast cavity), lined by the exocoelomic membrane, <b>nourishes</b> the embryo.","w":["Amniotic cavity contains it.","Trophoblast.","Primitive node cells.","Notochord."],"k":"Primary yolk sac = nutrition.","rn":[["","What is the main job of the Primary Yolk Sac?","What is the main job of the Primary Yolk Sac?","Nutrition; Nourishes the differentiating cells and embryo"]]},{"id":4031,"t":"Part 2 - Week 2: Implantation and the Bilaminar Disc","s":"On days 12–13, small extra-embryonic coelom clefts merge. What do they form, and what is the primary yolk sac then called?","o":["The chorionic cavity; the secondary yolk sac","The amniotic cavity; the secondary yolk sac","The chorionic cavity; the umbilical cord","The blastocoele; the secondary yolk sac","The amniotic cavity; the primary yolk sac"],"e":"<b>Extra-embryonic coelom clefts form and merge = chorionic cavity</b>. When this happens the primary yolk sac is renamed the <b>secondary yolk sac</b>.","w":["Merged clefts form the chorionic cavity.","The yolk sac is renamed the secondary yolk sac.","The blastocoele is from the blastocyst.","Wrong cavity and name."],"k":"Days 12–13: chorionic cavity; secondary yolk sac.","rn":[["","When does the primary yolk sac become the secondary yolk sac?","When does the primary yolk sac become the secondary yolk sac?","When the extra-embryonic coelom clefts unite; They form the chorionic cavity"]]},{"id":4032,"t":"Part 2 - Week 2: Implantation and the Bilaminar Disc","s":"Name the structure that develops into the umbilical cord.","e":"By days 12–13 the embryo hangs in the chorionic cavity by the connecting stalk, which becomes the umbilical cord.","k":"Connecting stalk.","a":["connecting stalk","the connecting stalk"],"rn":[["","Connecting Stalk","Connecting Stalk","Develops into the umbilical cord; The embryo hangs from it in the chorionic cavity"]]},{"id":4033,"t":"Part 2 - Week 2: Implantation and the Bilaminar Disc","s":"By the end of week 2, which description of the conceptus is correct?","o":["A flat two-layer disc between the amniotic cavity and yolk sac, on a stalk","A flat three-layer disc between the amniotic cavity and yolk sac, on a stalk","A flat two-layer disc inside the blastocoele, with no other cavities","A two-layer disc folded into a tube, inside the chorionic cavity","A solid ball of 12–16 cells inside the zona pellucida"],"e":"End of week 2: a <b>flat two-layer disc</b> (epiblast on top, hypoblast below) with the <b>amniotic cavity</b> above, the <b>yolk sac</b> below, hanging on a <b>stalk</b> inside the <b>chorionic cavity</b>.","w":["The trilaminar disc forms in week 3.","By week 2 there are amniotic, yolk and chorionic cavities.","Folding is week 4.","That is the morula."],"k":"Week 2 end: bilaminar disc, two “balloons”, stalk, chorionic cavity.","rn":[["","Hypoblast","Hypoblast","Lower layer of the bilaminar disc; Forms the exocoelomic membrane; Its cavity is the primary yolk sac"],["","Epiblast","Epiblast","Upper layer of the bilaminar disc; Forms the amnion (maternal side); Its cavity is the amniotic cavity"]]},{"id":4034,"t":"Part 2 - Week 2: Implantation and the Bilaminar Disc","f":"em15","s":"In the bilaminar disc, which layer lies closest to the old blastocoele?","o":["Hypoblast","Epiblast","Syncytiotrophoblast","Amnion","Ectoderm"],"e":"The <b>hypoblast</b> (yellow in the diagrams) lies closest to the old blastocoele; the <b>epiblast</b> (green) lies nearer the wall formed by the cytotrophoblast and syncytiotrophoblast.","w":["Nearer the trophoblast wall.","Outer trophoblast layer.","Membrane of the amniotic cavity.","Week 3."],"k":"Hypoblast faces the blastocoele.","rn":[["","Which bilaminar disc layer is closest to the blastocoele cavity?","Which bilaminar disc layer is closest to the blastocoele cavity?","Hypoblast"]]},{"id":4035,"t":"Part 2 - Week 3: Gastrulation","s":"Name the midline depression that appears at the caudal end of the bilaminar disc on day 15.","e":"The primitive streak appears on day 15 at the caudal end; the primitive node forms on day 16 at its cephalic end.","k":"Primitive streak.","a":["primitive streak"],"rn":[["","Primitive Streak","Primitive Streak","Midline depression; Caudal end of the bilaminar disc; Day 15"],["","Primitive Node","Primitive Node","At the cephalic (head) end of the primitive streak; Day 16"]]},{"id":4036,"t":"Part 2 - Week 3: Gastrulation","f":"em17","s":"Where is the primitive node located, and when does it appear?","o":["At the cephalic end of the streak, on day 16","At the caudal end of the streak, on day 15","At the cephalic end of the streak, on day 15","At the caudal end of the streak, on day 16","At the side of the neural tube, on day 20"],"e":"<b>Primitive node (day 16)</b> – <b>cephalic end</b> of the primitive streak.","w":["That is the streak.","Day 16.","The cephalic end.","Somites appear there on day 20."],"k":"Primitive node: cephalic end, day 16.","rn":[["","Primitive Node","Primitive Node","At the cephalic (head) end of the primitive streak; Day 16"]]},{"id":4037,"t":"Part 2 - Week 3: Gastrulation","s":"What is gastrulation?","o":["Conversion of the bilaminar disc into the trilaminar disc in week 3","Conversion of the morula into the blastocyst in week 1","Conversion of the neural plate into the neural tube in week 4","Conversion of the trophoblast into two layers in week 2","Conversion of the flat disc into a folded embryo in week 4"],"e":"<b>Gastrulation</b> turns the bilaminar disc into the <b>trilaminar disc</b> (ectoderm, mesoderm, endoderm) in <b>week 3</b>.","w":["That is blastocyst formation.","That is neurulation (days 18–25).","That is implantation.","That is folding."],"k":"Gastrulation: bilaminar → trilaminar (week 3).","rn":[["","Gastrulation","Gastrulation","Bilaminar disc becomes the trilaminar disc; Two layers to three layers; Week 3"]]},{"id":4038,"t":"Part 2 - Week 3: Gastrulation","s":"Where do the embryonic mesoderm and most of the embryonic endoderm come from?","o":["Migrating epiblast cells via the primitive streak","Hypoblast cells passing through the primitive streak","Migrating epiblast cells passing through the notochord","Trophoblast cells migrating into the disc","Hypoblast cells lining the yolk sac"],"e":"<b>Ectoderm cells migrate</b> (in response to formation of the primitive streak) to form <b>embryonic mesoderm and embryonic endoderm</b>. Most endoderm and all mesoderm come from migrating epiblast cells, <b>not</b> the hypoblast.","w":["The hypoblast forms umbilical vesicle endoderm.","They migrate in response to the primitive streak.","The trophoblast forms the placenta.","That is umbilical vesicle endoderm only."],"k":"Mesoderm + embryonic endoderm ← migrating epiblast cells.","rn":[["","Where does most embryonic endoderm come from?","Where does most embryonic endoderm come from?","Migrating ectoderm cells"],["","What makes ectoderm cells migrate?","What makes ectoderm cells migrate?","Formation of the primitive streak"],["","Where do migrating ectoderm cells go?","Where do migrating ectoderm cells go?","Between ectoderm and hypoblast; Form mesoderm and endoderm"]]},{"id":4039,"t":"Part 2 - Week 3: Gastrulation","s":"What does the hypoblast form during gastrulation?","o":["Umbilical vesicle endoderm","All of the embryonic endoderm","The embryonic mesoderm","The ectoderm of the embryo","The amnion lining"],"e":"<b>Hypoblast</b> → <b>endoderm of the umbilical vesicle</b> (secondary yolk sac). The epiblast forms ectoderm, and migrating ectoderm forms embryonic mesoderm and endoderm.","w":["Most embryonic endoderm is from migrating epiblast.","Mesoderm comes from migrating epiblast.","The epiblast forms ectoderm.","Not the hypoblast fate."],"k":"Hypoblast → umbilical vesicle endoderm.","rn":[["","What does the hypoblast form?","What does the hypoblast form?","Endoderm of the umbilical vesicle"]]},{"id":4040,"t":"Part 2 - Week 2: Implantation and the Bilaminar Disc","s":"What is the umbilical vesicle, and what are its roles?","o":["Secondary yolk sac; nutrition and forming the gut and airways","Secondary yolk sac; cushioning the embryo in amniotic fluid","Amniotic cavity; nutrition and forming the GI tract","Chorionic cavity; forming the foetal placenta","Primary yolk sac; forming the umbilical cord"],"e":"The <b>umbilical vesicle</b> (secondary yolk sac) is incorporated into the umbilical cord, connecting with the <b>midgut</b> when the embryo folds. It provides <b>nutrition</b> and helps form the <b>respiratory and gastrointestinal tracts</b>.","w":["Cushioning was not its role.","It is the yolk sac, not the amniotic cavity.","The trophoblast forms the placenta.","The umbilical vesicle is the secondary yolk sac."],"k":"Umbilical vesicle = secondary yolk sac (nutrition; gut/airway).","rn":[["","What does the umbilical vesicle (secondary yolk sac) do?","What does the umbilical vesicle (secondary yolk sac) do?","Provides nutrition to the embryo; Helps form respiratory and gastrointestinal tracts"]]},{"id":4041,"t":"Part 2 - Week 3: Gastrulation","s":"From where do the cells that form the notochordal plate migrate, and in which direction?","o":["Cranially from the primitive node","Caudally from the primitive streak","Laterally from the somites","Ventrally from the neural tube","From the trophoblast towards the epiblast"],"e":"The <b>notochordal plate/notochord (days 17–21)</b> forms from <b>cranial migration of cells from the primitive node</b>.","w":["Opposite direction and origin.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Notochord: cells migrate cranially from the primitive node.","rn":[["","Where do notochord cells come from?","Where do notochord cells come from?","Cranial migration from primitive node"]]},{"id":4042,"t":"Part 2 - Week 3: Gastrulation","s":"Which statement about the notochord is correct?","o":["It forms the axis and releases mediators driving migration","It forms the embryo’s longitudinal axis and becomes the neural tube","It forms the embryo’s longitudinal axis and becomes the umbilical cord","It is a hollow tube that later fills with cerebrospinal fluid","It forms at the caudal end on day 15 as a midline depression"],"e":"The notochord <b>forms the longitudinal axis</b> of the embryo (a solid cylinder) and <b>releases soluble mediators</b> that help form the neural groove and drive migration of ectoderm cells.","w":["The neural tube forms from ectoderm.","The connecting stalk forms the cord.","That is the neural tube.","That is the primitive streak."],"k":"Notochord: longitudinal axis, signalling.","rn":[["","What does the notochord form?","What does the notochord form?","Longitudinal axis of the embryo"]]},{"id":4043,"t":"Part 2 - Week 3: Gastrulation","f":"em18","s":"In a cross-section of the trilaminar disc, where does the notochord lie?","o":["In the midline, between the neural tube and the developing aorta","In the midline, above the neural tube in the amniotic cavity","Lateral to the paraxial mesoderm, beside the intermediate mesoderm","In the midline, within the endoderm lining the gut","Lateral to the neural tube, on each side of it"],"e":"In the cross-sections the notochord is the dot <b>between the neural tube and the developing aorta</b>, in the <b>midline</b>.","w":["It lies below the neural tube.","That is intermediate mesoderm territory.","It is between the tube and aorta.","Somites lie either side."],"k":"Notochord: midline, between neural tube and aorta.","rn":[["","What does the notochord stimulate?","What does the notochord stimulate?","Migration of cells; Neural groove formation; Neural tube development"]]},{"id":4044,"t":"Part 2 - Week 3: Gastrulation","s":"Name the process that forms the neural tube from the neural plate between days 18 and 25.","e":"Neurulation: neural plate → neural groove with neural folds → neural tube, later filled with CSF; dilation forms the brain and ventricles.","k":"Neurulation.","a":["neurulation"],"rn":[["","Neurulation","Neurulation","Formation of the neural tube; Days 18-25"],["","What are the 3 stages of neurulation?","What are the 3 stages of neurulation?","Neural plate (day 18); Neural groove with neural folds (day 20); Neural tube (day 22)"]]},{"id":4045,"t":"Part 2 - Week 3: Gastrulation","s":"What is the neural tube filled with later, and what do its dilation and folding form?","o":["Cerebrospinal fluid; the brain and ventricles","Cerebrospinal fluid; the spinal nerves","Amniotic fluid; the brain and ventricles","Blood; the heart and great vessels","Cerebrospinal fluid; the somites"],"e":"The neural tube is a <b>hollow</b> structure later filled with <b>cerebrospinal fluid</b>; <b>dilation and folding</b> form the <b>brain and ventricles</b>.","w":["Dilation and folding form the brain and ventricles.","It fills with CSF.","Not described.","Somites are paraxial mesoderm."],"k":"Neural tube: CSF; dilates to brain and ventricles.","rn":[["","What fills the hollow neural tube?","What fills the hollow neural tube?","Cerebrospinal fluid"]]},{"id":4046,"t":"Part 2 - Week 3: Gastrulation","s":"Name the primary brain vesicle that does NOT divide into two secondary vesicles.","e":"Prosencephalon → telencephalon + diencephalon; rhombencephalon → metencephalon + myelencephalon; the mesencephalon stays as one.","k":"Mesencephalon.","a":["mesencephalon","midbrain"],"rn":[["","What are the 5 secondary brain vesicles?","What are the 5 secondary brain vesicles?","Telencephalon; Diencephalon; Mesencephalon; Metencephalon; Myelencephalon"],["","What are the 3 primary brain vesicles (cranial to caudal)?","What are the 3 primary brain vesicles (cranial to caudal)?","Prosencephalon; Mesencephalon; Rhombencephalon"]]},{"id":4047,"t":"Part 2 - Week 3: Gastrulation","f":"em21","s":"The prosencephalon divides into which two secondary vesicles?","o":["Telencephalon and diencephalon","Metencephalon and myelencephalon","Mesencephalon and diencephalon","Telencephalon and metencephalon","Diencephalon and myelencephalon"],"e":"<b>Prosencephalon</b> (forebrain) → <b>telencephalon and diencephalon</b>. Rhombencephalon → metencephalon and myelencephalon. Mesencephalon stays as one.","w":["Rhombencephalon.","Mesencephalon stays single.","Mixed.","Mixed."],"k":"Prosencephalon → tel- + diencephalon.","rn":[["","What does the prosencephalon divide into?","What does the prosencephalon divide into?","Telencephalon; Diencephalon"],["","What are the 5 secondary brain vesicles?","What are the 5 secondary brain vesicles?","Telencephalon; Diencephalon; Mesencephalon; Metencephalon; Myelencephalon"]]},{"id":4048,"t":"Part 2 - Week 3: Gastrulation","s":"Which primary vesicle does <b>not</b> divide when the 3 primary vesicles become 5 secondary vesicles?","o":["Mesencephalon","Prosencephalon","Rhombencephalon","Telencephalon","Myelencephalon"],"e":"“3 → 5”: the <b>front</b> (pros-) and <b>back</b> (rhomb-) vesicles each split in two; the <b>middle</b> (<b>mesencephalon</b>) stays as one.","w":["Splits into tel- and diencephalon.","Splits into met- and myelencephalon.","Secondary vesicle.","Secondary vesicle."],"k":"Mesencephalon stays single.","rn":[["","Which primary brain vesicle does not divide further?","Which primary brain vesicle does not divide further?","Mesencephalon"],["","What are the 3 primary brain vesicles (cranial to caudal)?","What are the 3 primary brain vesicles (cranial to caudal)?","Prosencephalon; Mesencephalon; Rhombencephalon"],["","What are the 5 secondary brain vesicles?","What are the 5 secondary brain vesicles?","Telencephalon; Diencephalon; Mesencephalon; Metencephalon; Myelencephalon"]]},{"id":4049,"t":"Part 2 - Week 3: Gastrulation","s":"When do the brain vesicles form, and what does the rest of the neural tube become?","o":["Weeks 3–5; the spinal cord","Weeks 9–38; the spinal nerves","Days 4–5; the notochord","Week 1; the cauda equina","Week 2; the amnion"],"e":"The brain forms from the neural tube as 3 primary → 5 secondary vesicles in <b>weeks 3–5</b>. The rest of the neural tube becomes the <b>spinal cord</b>.","w":["Foetal period.","Blastocyst.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Vesicles weeks 3–5; rest of tube → spinal cord.","rn":[["","What does neurulation form from the neural tube?","What does neurulation form from the neural tube?","Brain and ventricles; Spinal cord (rest of the tube)"]]},{"id":4050,"t":"Part 2 - Week 3: Gastrulation","s":"Which secondary vesicles come from the rhombencephalon?","o":["Metencephalon and myelencephalon","Telencephalon and diencephalon","Mesencephalon only","Diencephalon and mesencephalon","Telencephalon and myelencephalon"],"e":"<b>Rhombencephalon</b> (hindbrain) → <b>metencephalon and myelencephalon</b>.","w":["Prosencephalon.","Unchanged midbrain.","Mixed.","Mixed."],"k":"Rhombencephalon → met- + myelencephalon.","rn":[["","What does the rhombencephalon divide into?","What does the rhombencephalon divide into?","Metencephalon; Myelencephalon"]]},{"id":4051,"t":"Germ Layers and Their Derivatives","s":"A congenital defect affects the epidermis, the nervous system and the inner ear. Which germ layer gives rise to all three?","o":["Ectoderm","Mesoderm","Endoderm","Hypoblast","Trophoblast"],"e":"<b>Ectoderm</b> forms the <b>epidermis</b>, the <b>viscerocranium</b> and the <b>nervous system</b>, including the <b>eyes and inner ear</b>.","w":["Muscle, skeleton, urogenital, serous membranes.","Epithelial linings and glands.","Bilaminar disc layer.","Placenta."],"k":"Ectoderm: epidermis, viscerocranium, nervous system (eyes, inner ear).","rn":[["","What does Ectoderm form?","What does Ectoderm form?","Epidermis; Viscerocranium; Nervous system (including eyes and inner ear)"]]},{"id":4052,"t":"Germ Layers and Their Derivatives","s":"The facial bones around the eyes, nose and mouth come from ectoderm. What is this part of the skull called?","e":"The viscerocranium is the bones of the skull that form the face, not those housing the brain; it comes from ectoderm.","k":"Viscerocranium.","a":["viscerocranium"],"rn":[["","What is the viscerocranium?","What is the viscerocranium?","Skull bones forming the face; Eyes, nose and mouth region; Not the bones housing the brain"]]},{"id":4053,"t":"Germ Layers and Their Derivatives","s":"On day 17 the mesoderm differentiates into three parts. List them from medial to lateral.","o":["Paraxial, intermediate, lateral plate","Lateral plate, intermediate, paraxial","Intermediate, paraxial, lateral plate","Paraxial, lateral plate, intermediate","Somatic, splanchnic, paraxial"],"e":"Day 17: <b>paraxial</b> (next to the axis), <b>intermediate</b> (slightly more lateral), <b>lateral plate</b> (most lateral).","w":["Reversed.","Paraxial is next to the axis.","Intermediate is in the middle.","These are lateral plate layers."],"k":"Mesoderm medial→lateral: paraxial, intermediate, lateral plate.","rn":[["","Intermediate Mesoderm","Intermediate Mesoderm","Slightly more lateral than paraxial mesoderm; Forms the urogenital system"]]},{"id":4054,"t":"Germ Layers and Their Derivatives","s":"A malformation affects skeletal muscle, the skeleton and the dermis. Which mesoderm subdivision is most likely involved?","o":["Paraxial mesoderm","Intermediate mesoderm","Lateral plate mesoderm","Endoderm","Ectoderm"],"e":"<b>Paraxial mesoderm</b> forms <b>skeletal muscle, skeleton, dermis and connective tissue</b> and divides into somites.","w":["Urogenital system.","Serous membranes, smooth muscle, stroma.","Linings and glands.","Epidermis, nervous system."],"k":"Paraxial mesoderm: skeletal muscle, skeleton, dermis.","rn":[["","What does Paraxial Mesoderm form?","What does Paraxial Mesoderm form?","Skeletal muscle; Majority of the skeleton; Dermis; Connective tissue"]]},{"id":4055,"t":"Germ Layers and Their Derivatives","s":"Which mesoderm subdivision gives rise to the urogenital system?","o":["Intermediate mesoderm","Paraxial mesoderm","Lateral plate mesoderm","Somatic layer","Sclerotome"],"e":"<b>Intermediate mesoderm</b> → <b>urogenital system</b> (“uro” = urinary: kidneys, ureters, bladder, urethra; “genital” = reproductive).","w":["Muscle and skeleton.","Serous membranes, smooth muscle.","Parietal membranes.","Vertebrae/skeleton."],"k":"Intermediate mesoderm → urogenital.","rn":[["","Intermediate Mesoderm","Intermediate Mesoderm","Slightly more lateral than paraxial mesoderm; Forms the urogenital system"],["","Which germ layers form the bladder?","Which germ layers form the bladder?","Intermediate mesoderm: the bladder; Endoderm: its mucosa"]]},{"id":4056,"t":"Germ Layers and Their Derivatives","s":"Which mesoderm subdivision forms the serous membranes, smooth muscle and stroma?","o":["Lateral plate mesoderm","Paraxial mesoderm","Intermediate mesoderm","Somites","Notochord"],"e":"<b>Lateral plate mesoderm</b> (most lateral group from the neural tube) forms <b>serous membranes</b> (somatic/parietal and splanchnic/visceral), <b>smooth muscle</b> and <b>stroma</b>.","w":["Skeletal muscle, skeleton.","Urogenital.","Paraxial derivatives.","Midline axis."],"k":"Lateral plate: serous membranes, smooth muscle, stroma.","rn":[["","What does Lateral Plate Mesoderm form?","What does Lateral Plate Mesoderm form?","Serous membranes; Smooth muscle; Stroma"]]},{"id":4057,"t":"Germ Layers and Their Derivatives","s":"Paraxial mesoderm forms muscle. Which type?","o":["Skeletal muscle only","Smooth muscle only","Cardiac muscle only","Smooth and cardiac muscle","All three muscle types"],"e":"The recording stressed that paraxial mesoderm forms <b>skeletal muscle only</b>, not smooth or cardiac muscle. Smooth muscle comes from <b>lateral plate</b> mesoderm.","w":["Lateral plate.","Not stated for paraxial.","Not paraxial.","Skeletal only."],"k":"Paraxial → skeletal muscle only.","rn":[["","Which type of muscle does paraxial mesoderm form?","Which type of muscle does paraxial mesoderm form?","Skeletal muscle; Not smooth or cardiac muscle"]]},{"id":4058,"t":"Germ Layers and Their Derivatives","s":"When do somites appear, and how many pairs form?","o":["Day 20; 42–44 pairs","Day 20; 31 pairs","Day 17; 42–44 pairs","Day 22; 33 pairs","Day 15; 42–44 pairs"],"e":"<b>Somites</b> appear on <b>day 20</b>, in <b>42–44 pairs</b>, either side of the notochord.","w":["31 is spinal nerves.","Day 17 is mesoderm differentiation.","Day 22 is neural tube.","Day 15 is the primitive streak."],"k":"Somites: day 20, 42–44 pairs.","rn":[["","How many pairs of somites form?","How many pairs of somites form?","42-44 pairs; Line up either side of the notochord"],["","When do somites appear?","When do somites appear?","Day 20"]]},{"id":4059,"t":"Germ Layers and Their Derivatives","s":"Each somite divides into a dermatomyotome and which other part, which forms the vertebrae?","e":"Somites divide into dermatomyotome (dermis and skeletal muscle) and sclerotome (vertebrae/skeleton).","k":"Sclerotome.","a":["sclerotome"],"rn":[["","What does each somite divide into?","What does each somite divide into?","Dermatomyotome; Sclerotome"],["","What does the Sclerotome form?","What does the Sclerotome form?","Vertebrae and skeleton (bone)"]]},{"id":4060,"t":"Germ Layers and Their Derivatives","s":"A fetus is found to have absent kidneys and abnormal gonads, with normal skeletal muscle and normal serous membranes. Failure of which labelled structure best explains this?","o":["B","A","C","D","E"],"e":"B is the intermediate mesoderm, slightly lateral to the paraxial mesoderm. It forms the urogenital system: urinary (kidneys, ureters, bladder, urethra) and reproductive organs. The skeleton and muscle (paraxial) and serous membranes (lateral plate) are spared.","w":["A (paraxial mesoderm) forms skeletal muscle, skeleton and dermis, which are normal.","C is the notochord, which forms the longitudinal axis and signals migration.","D (somatic layer) forms parietal serous membranes, which are normal.","E (splanchnic layer) forms visceral membranes and smooth muscle around the gut."],"k":"Intermediate mesoderm → urogenital system.","img":{"src":"4/mesoderm21","cap":"Transverse section of an embryo at 21 days. Labels removed."},"rn":[["","Intermediate Mesoderm","Intermediate Mesoderm","Slightly more lateral than paraxial mesoderm; Forms the urogenital system"],["","What does Mesoderm form?","What does Mesoderm form?","Skeletal muscle; Skeleton; Connective tissue; Urogenital system; Serous membranes"]]},{"id":4061,"t":"Germ Layers and Their Derivatives","f":"em25","s":"The parietal pleura lines the wall of the thoracic cavity. From which layer of the lateral plate mesoderm does it come?","o":["Somatic (parietal) layer","Splanchnic (visceral) layer","Intermediate mesoderm","Paraxial mesoderm","Endoderm"],"e":"The <b>somatic/parietal layer</b> forms the <b>parietal membrane</b> lining the <b>body cavity wall</b> (e.g. parietal pleura). The splanchnic/visceral layer forms visceral membranes covering organs (e.g. visceral pleura).","w":["Covers the organs.","Urogenital.","Muscle and skeleton.","Epithelial linings."],"k":"Somatic layer → parietal membranes (body wall).","rn":[["","What does the somatic (parietal) layer form?","What does the somatic (parietal) layer form?","Parietal membrane; Lines the body cavity wall"]]},{"id":4062,"t":"Germ Layers and Their Derivatives","f":"em25","s":"The visceral pleura covers the surface of the lung. From which layer is it derived?","o":["Splanchnic (visceral) layer of lateral plate mesoderm","Somatic (parietal) layer of lateral plate mesoderm","Splanchnic (visceral) layer of paraxial mesoderm","Endoderm of the respiratory tract","Intermediate mesoderm"],"e":"The <b>splanchnic/visceral layer</b> wraps the gut and organs, forming <b>visceral membranes</b> such as the <b>visceral pleura</b>.","w":["Somatic forms parietal membranes.","Paraxial forms somites.","Endoderm forms lung tissue/epithelium, not pleura.","Intermediate forms urogenital."],"k":"Splanchnic layer → visceral membranes (organs).","rn":[["","What does the splanchnic (visceral) layer form?","What does the splanchnic (visceral) layer form?","Visceral membrane; Covers the organs"]]},{"id":4063,"t":"Germ Layers and Their Derivatives","s":"What is “stroma”, which is formed by lateral plate mesoderm?","o":["The supporting tissue of organs, not the organ tissue itself","The functional cells of an organ, not its supporting tissue","The innermost epithelial lining of hollow organs","The serous membrane covering each organ","The smooth muscle layer of the gut wall"],"e":"<b>Stroma</b> is the <b>supporting tissue</b> of organs, not the organ tissue itself. Lateral plate mesoderm also forms smooth muscle.","w":["That is parenchyma.","That is endoderm.","Serous membranes are separate.","Smooth muscle is listed separately."],"k":"Stroma = supporting tissue.","rn":[["","What is stroma?","What is stroma?","Supporting tissue of an organ; Not the organ tissue itself"]]},{"id":4064,"t":"Germ Layers and Their Derivatives","s":"In a hollow organ, which part comes from endoderm and which from mesoderm?","o":["Lining from endoderm; stroma and smooth muscle from mesoderm","Lining from mesoderm; stroma and smooth muscle from endoderm","Lining from ectoderm; stroma and smooth muscle from mesoderm","Lining and smooth muscle from endoderm; stroma from mesoderm","Lining, stroma and smooth muscle all from endoderm"],"e":"In an organ: <b>stroma + smooth muscle</b> come from <b>mesoderm</b>, but the <b>innermost lining (epithelium)</b> comes from <b>endoderm</b>.","w":["Reversed.","The lining is endoderm.","Smooth muscle is mesoderm.","Stroma and smooth muscle are mesoderm."],"k":"Organ: epithelium (endoderm) + stroma/smooth muscle (mesoderm).","rn":[["","Which germ layers make smooth muscle, stroma and the inner lining of viscera?","Which germ layers make smooth muscle, stroma and the inner lining of viscera?","Lateral plate mesoderm: smooth muscle, stroma; Endoderm: epithelium (inner lining)"]]},{"id":4065,"t":"Germ Layers and Their Derivatives","f":"em26","s":"Which of the following comes from endoderm?","o":["Liver parenchymal cells","Epidermis","Skeletal muscle","Serous membranes","Dermis"],"e":"Endoderm forms the epithelium of the alimentary and respiratory tracts, <b>parenchymal cells of the liver, pancreas, thyroid and parathyroids</b>, and the <b>mucosa of the bladder</b>.","w":["Ectoderm.","Paraxial mesoderm.","Lateral plate mesoderm.","Paraxial mesoderm."],"k":"Endoderm: liver/pancreas/thyroid/parathyroid parenchyma.","rn":[["","What does Endoderm form?","What does Endoderm form?","Epithelium of the alimentary tract; Epithelium and glands of the respiratory tract; Parenchymal cells (liver, pancreas, thyroid, parathyroid); Mucosa of the bladder; Lung tissue (parenchyma)"]]},{"id":4066,"t":"Germ Layers and Their Derivatives","s":"The bladder as an organ belongs to the urogenital system. Which part of the bladder comes from endoderm?","o":["Its mucosa","Its smooth muscle wall","Its supporting stroma","Its serous covering","The whole bladder"],"e":"The bladder belongs to the urogenital system (intermediate mesoderm), but its <b>mucosa</b> (innermost lining) comes from <b>endoderm</b>.","w":["Mesoderm.","Mesoderm.","Lateral plate mesoderm.","The organ is urogenital (intermediate mesoderm)."],"k":"Bladder mucosa = endoderm.","rn":[["","Which germ layers form the bladder?","Which germ layers form the bladder?","Intermediate mesoderm: the bladder; Endoderm: its mucosa"]]},{"id":4067,"t":"Germ Layers and Their Derivatives","s":"Which part of the lungs comes from endoderm?","o":["Lung tissue (parenchyma), not the bronchial tree","The bronchial tree, not the lung tissue","The visceral pleura covering the lung","The smooth muscle of the airways","Both the lung tissue and the bronchial tree"],"e":"For the lungs, it is the actual <b>lung tissue (parenchyma)</b>, <b>not the bronchial tree</b>, that the lecturer attributes to endoderm. Endoderm also forms the epithelium and glands of the respiratory tract.","w":["Reversed.","Pleura is lateral plate mesoderm.","Mesoderm.","The lecturer said not the bronchial tree."],"k":"Lung parenchyma = endoderm.","rn":[["","What does Endoderm form?","What does Endoderm form?","Epithelium of the alimentary tract; Epithelium and glands of the respiratory tract; Parenchymal cells (liver, pancreas, thyroid, parathyroid); Mucosa of the bladder; Lung tissue (parenchyma)"]]},{"id":4068,"t":"Germ Layers and Their Derivatives","s":"Which list contains <b>only</b> endodermal derivatives?","o":["Alimentary epithelium, pancreas parenchyma, bladder mucosa","Alimentary epithelium, pancreas parenchyma, bladder smooth muscle","Alimentary epithelium, epidermis, bladder mucosa","Thyroid parenchyma, visceral pleura, bladder mucosa","Liver parenchyma, dermis, respiratory epithelium"],"e":"Endoderm: <b>alimentary tract epithelium</b>, <b>respiratory tract epithelium and glands</b>, <b>liver, pancreas, thyroid, parathyroid parenchyma</b>, <b>bladder mucosa</b>, lung tissue.","w":["Smooth muscle is mesoderm.","Epidermis is ectoderm.","Pleura is mesoderm.","Dermis is paraxial mesoderm."],"k":"Endoderm = inner linings + glandular parenchyma.","rn":[["","What does Endoderm form?","What does Endoderm form?","Epithelium of the alimentary tract; Epithelium and glands of the respiratory tract; Parenchymal cells (liver, pancreas, thyroid, parathyroid); Mucosa of the bladder; Lung tissue (parenchyma)"]]},{"id":4069,"t":"Germ Layers and Their Derivatives","s":"Which pairing of structure and germ layer is <b>incorrect</b>?","o":["Epidermis – mesoderm","Inner ear – ectoderm","Urogenital system – mesoderm","Thyroid parenchyma – endoderm","Serous membranes – mesoderm"],"e":"The <b>epidermis</b> is from <b>ectoderm</b>. (The <b>dermis</b> comes from paraxial mesoderm.)","w":["Correct.","Correct.","Correct.","Correct."],"k":"Epidermis = ectoderm; dermis = mesoderm.","rn":[["","What is the dermis?","What is the dermis?","Layer immediately beneath the epidermis"],["","Mesoderm","Mesoderm","Middle germ layer; From migrating ectoderm cells"]]},{"id":4070,"t":"Germ Layers and Their Derivatives","s":"A tissue sample shows skin. The superficial layer and the deeper supporting layer come from different germ layers. Which is correct?","o":["Epidermis from ectoderm; dermis from paraxial mesoderm","Epidermis from ectoderm; dermis from lateral plate mesoderm","Epidermis from paraxial mesoderm; dermis from ectoderm","Epidermis from endoderm; dermis from paraxial mesoderm","Epidermis and dermis both from ectoderm"],"e":"<b>Epidermis</b> → <b>ectoderm</b>. <b>Dermis</b> (just beneath the epidermis) → <b>paraxial mesoderm</b> (dermatome).","w":["Dermis is from the dermatome (paraxial).","Reversed.","Epidermis is ectoderm.","Dermis is mesoderm."],"k":"Skin = ectoderm (epidermis) + mesoderm (dermis).","rn":[["","What is the dermis?","What is the dermis?","Layer immediately beneath the epidermis"],["","What does Paraxial Mesoderm form?","What does Paraxial Mesoderm form?","Skeletal muscle; Majority of the skeleton; Dermis; Connective tissue"]]},{"id":4071,"t":"Part 2 - Week 3: Gastrulation","s":"Name the midline structure labelled <b>C</b>, which forms the embryo’s longitudinal axis and releases signals that drive cell migration.","e":"C is the notochord, the small midline rod between the neural tube and the aorta. It forms from cells migrating cranially from the primitive node (days 17–21), defines the longitudinal axis, and releases soluble mediators that drive neural groove formation and germ-layer migration.","k":"C = notochord.","img":{"src":"4/mesoderm21","cap":"Transverse section of an embryo at 21 days. Labels removed."},"a":["notochord"],"rn":[["","What does the notochord form?","What does the notochord form?","Longitudinal axis of the embryo"],["","What does the notochord stimulate?","What does the notochord stimulate?","Migration of cells; Neural groove formation; Neural tube development"],["","When does the notochord form?","When does the notochord form?","Days 17-21"]]},{"id":4072,"t":"Part 2 - Week 3: Gastrulation","f":"em18","s":"In the trilaminar disc, which order of layers is correct from top (amniotic side) to bottom?","o":["Ectoderm, mesoderm, endoderm","Endoderm, mesoderm, ectoderm","Mesoderm, ectoderm, endoderm","Ectoderm, endoderm, mesoderm","Epiblast, mesoderm, hypoblast"],"e":"Three stacked layers: <b>ectoderm</b> on top, <b>mesoderm</b> in the middle, <b>endoderm</b> underneath, with the notochord in the midline.","w":["Reversed.","Mesoderm is in the middle.","Mesoderm is in the middle.","Trilaminar layers are ecto/meso/endoderm."],"k":"Ecto (top), meso (middle), endo (bottom).","rn":[["","What are the 3 layers of the trilaminar disc?","What are the 3 layers of the trilaminar disc?","Ectoderm; Mesoderm; Endoderm"],["","Mesoderm","Mesoderm","Middle germ layer; From migrating ectoderm cells"]]},{"id":4073,"t":"Week 1 of Development​","s":"In which week does the embryo fold, and is it still an embryo at this time?","o":["Week 4; still an embryo","Week 4; already a foetus","Week 9; just become a foetus","Week 3; still pre-embryonic","Week 8; just become a foetus"],"e":"Folding happens in <b>week 4</b>: we are <b>still in the embryonic period</b>, not yet a foetus.","w":["The foetal period starts at week 9.","Folding is week 4.","Pre-embryonic ends at week 2.","Folding is week 4."],"k":"Folding: week 4, embryonic period.","rn":[["","Why are ventral, dorsal, cranial and caudal used in the embryo?","Why are ventral, dorsal, cranial and caudal used in the embryo?","Embryo is not yet in anatomical position; Structures and limbs are still developing"]]},{"id":4074,"t":"Week 4: Folding of the Embryo","s":"What drives longitudinal (cephalocaudal) folding?","o":["Enlargement of the brain vesicles at the cranial end","Development of the somites along each side","Merging of the extra-embryonic coelom clefts","Growth of the primitive streak at the caudal end","Elongation of the notochord along the midline"],"e":"<b>Longitudinal folding (days 21–24)</b> is driven by <b>enlargement of the brain vesicles</b> at the cranial end (neurulation).","w":["Somites drive lateral folding.","That forms the chorionic cavity.","Not described.","Not the cause given."],"k":"Longitudinal folding ← brain vesicles.","rn":[["","What causes longitudinal folding?","What causes longitudinal folding?","Enlargement of the brain vesicles; Neurulation (brain and spinal cord)"]]},{"id":4075,"t":"Week 4: Folding of the Embryo","s":"What drives lateral (transverse) folding, and when does it start?","o":["Somite development; day 20 onwards","Brain vesicle enlargement; days 21–24","Notochord formation; day 17","Neural crest migration; day 15","Gastrulation; day 16"],"e":"<b>Lateral folding (day 20+)</b> is driven by <b>somite development</b> (42–44 pairs).","w":["Longitudinal.","Not the driver.","Not stated.","Not the driver."],"k":"Lateral folding ← somites (day 20+).","rn":[["","When does lateral folding start?","When does lateral folding start?","Day 20 onwards"]]},{"id":4076,"t":"Week 4: Folding of the Embryo","f":"em27","s":"What is the result of lateral folding?","o":["The sides meet ventrally, closing the future peritoneal cavity","The head and tail curl under to form foregut, midgut and hindgut","The sides meet dorsally, closing the neural tube","The sides meet ventrally, closing the amniotic cavity","The sides meet ventrally, closing the chorionic cavity"],"e":"In lateral folding the sides fold <b>ventrally and meet</b>, <b>closing the body cavity</b> (future <b>peritoneal cavity</b>), enclosing the gut.","w":["That is longitudinal folding.","That is neurulation.","It closes the body (peritoneal) cavity.","It closes the body cavity."],"k":"Lateral folding closes the body cavity.","rn":[["","What does lateral folding do?","What does lateral folding do?","Sides fold ventrally and meet; Closes off the body cavity"]]},{"id":4077,"t":"Week 4: Folding of the Embryo","f":"em28","s":"Longitudinal folding pinches the yolk sac into the gut. Which part of the gut remains connected to the yolk sac, and by what?","o":["The midgut, by the vitello-intestinal duct","The foregut, by the vitello-intestinal duct","The hindgut, by the vitello-intestinal duct","The midgut, by the connecting stalk","The foregut, by the connecting stalk"],"e":"Head and tail folds curl under, forming <b>foregut, midgut and hindgut</b>. The <b>midgut</b> stays connected to the yolk sac by the <b>vitello-intestinal duct</b>.","w":["The midgut stays connected.","The midgut stays connected.","The duct, not the stalk.","Wrong part and connection."],"k":"Midgut ↔ yolk sac via vitello-intestinal duct.","rn":[["","What joins the midgut to the yolk sac?","What joins the midgut to the yolk sac?","Vitello-intestinal duct"]]},{"id":4078,"t":"Germ Layers and Their Derivatives","s":"Layer <b>D</b> follows the body wall. Which adult structure develops from it?","o":["Parietal pleura lining the thoracic wall","Visceral pleura covering the surface of the lung","Epithelium lining the respiratory tract","Dermis of the skin of the chest wall","Skeletal muscle of the intercostal spaces"],"e":"D is the somatic (parietal) layer of lateral plate mesoderm, which follows the body wall and forms parietal serous membranes, e.g. the parietal pleura lining the thoracic cavity. The splanchnic (visceral) layer around the gut forms visceral membranes such as the visceral pleura.","w":["Visceral pleura comes from the splanchnic layer that wraps the gut and organs.","Respiratory epithelium comes from endoderm.","Dermis comes from paraxial mesoderm (dermatome).","Skeletal muscle comes from paraxial mesoderm (myotome)."],"k":"Somatic layer → parietal membranes.","img":{"src":"4/mesoderm21","cap":"Transverse section of an embryo at 21 days. Labels removed."},"rn":[["","What are parietal and visceral pleura?","What are parietal and visceral pleura?","Parietal: lines the pleural (thoracic) cavity; Visceral: surrounds the lung"],["","What does the somatic (parietal) layer form?","What does the somatic (parietal) layer form?","Parietal membrane; Lines the body cavity wall"]]},{"id":4079,"t":"Germ Layers and Their Derivatives","s":"After lateral folding closes the body cavity, which tissue lines this cavity?","o":["Serous membranes from lateral plate mesoderm","Serous membranes from paraxial mesoderm","Epithelium from the endoderm","Epidermis from the ectoderm","Serous membranes from intermediate mesoderm"],"e":"The cavity that becomes the abdominal/peritoneal cavity is lined by the <b>serous membranes</b> from the <b>lateral plate mesoderm</b>.","w":["Paraxial forms somites.","Endoderm lines the gut.","Ectoderm is the outside.","Intermediate forms urogenital."],"k":"Body cavity lined by lateral plate serous membranes.","rn":[["","What does Lateral Plate Mesoderm form?","What does Lateral Plate Mesoderm form?","Serous membranes; Smooth muscle; Stroma"]]},{"id":4080,"t":"Periods of Development","s":"Which sequence of events is in the correct chronological order?","o":["Morula → blastocyst → implantation → bilaminar disc → primitive streak","Blastocyst → morula → implantation → bilaminar disc → primitive streak","Morula → blastocyst → bilaminar disc → implantation → primitive streak","Morula → implantation → blastocyst → bilaminar disc → primitive streak","Morula → blastocyst → implantation → primitive streak → bilaminar disc"],"e":"Day 3 morula → days 4–5 blastocyst → days 6–13 implantation → days 8–10 bilaminar disc → day 15 primitive streak.","w":["The morula comes first.","Implantation begins (day 6) before the bilaminar disc (days 8–10).","The blastocyst implants.","The streak forms in the bilaminar disc."],"k":"Know the week 1–3 sequence.","rn":[["","What happens in the pre-embryonic period?","What happens in the pre-embryonic period?","Fertilisation; Morula and blastocyst form; Blastocyst implants; Bilaminar disc forms"],["","Primitive Streak","Primitive Streak","Midline depression; Caudal end of the bilaminar disc; Day 15"]]},{"id":4081,"t":"Germ Layers and Their Derivatives","s":"Which event happens on day 17?","o":["Mesoderm splits into paraxial, intermediate, lateral plate","Somites appear either side of the notochord","The primitive streak appears at the caudal end","The neural tube closes in the midline","The primitive node appears at the cephalic end"],"e":"<b>Day 17</b>: mesoderm differentiates (paraxial, intermediate, lateral plate). The notochord forms days 17–21.","w":["Day 20.","Day 15.","Day 22.","Day 16."],"k":"Day 17: mesoderm subdivisions.","rn":[["","Intermediate Mesoderm","Intermediate Mesoderm","Slightly more lateral than paraxial mesoderm; Forms the urogenital system"]]},{"id":4082,"t":"Part 2 - Week 3: Gastrulation","s":"Which pairing of event and timing is <b>incorrect</b>?","o":["Neurulation – days 4–5","Primitive node – day 16","Somites appear – day 20","Notochord – days 17–21","Brain vesicles – weeks 3–5"],"e":"<b>Neurulation</b> occurs on <b>days 18–25</b>. Days 4–5 is blastocyst formation.","w":["Correct.","Correct.","Correct.","Correct."],"k":"Neurulation: days 18–25.","rn":[["","Neurulation","Neurulation","Formation of the neural tube; Days 18-25"]]},{"id":4083,"t":"Part 2 - Week 3: Gastrulation","s":"An embryo has a neural groove with neural folds but no closed neural tube yet. About what age is it?","o":["About day 20","About day 10","About day 30","About day 3","About week 9"],"e":"Neural plate day 18 → <b>neural groove with neural folds day 20</b> → neural tube day 22.","w":["Bilaminar disc.","Neural tube formed.","Morula.","Foetus."],"k":"Neural groove ~day 20.","rn":[["","What are the 3 stages of neurulation?","What are the 3 stages of neurulation?","Neural plate (day 18); Neural groove with neural folds (day 20); Neural tube (day 22)"]]},{"id":4084,"t":"Week 4: Folding of the Embryo","s":"An embryo has a single flat disc of two layers, a primitive streak at its caudal end and a node at the streak’s cephalic end, but no mesoderm yet between the layers. Which statement is correct?","o":["It is about day 16, just starting gastrulation","It is about day 10, still a simple bilaminar disc","It is about day 25, folding longitudinally","It is about day 16, and the hypoblast will form the mesoderm","It is about day 20, with somites already formed"],"e":"The primitive streak appears on day 15 and the node on day 16. Gastrulation then converts the bilaminar disc into the trilaminar disc: epiblast cells migrate through the streak to form mesoderm and most of the endoderm.","w":["At day 10 there is no primitive streak.","By day 25 the embryo has a trilaminar disc and is folding.","Mesoderm comes from migrating epiblast (ectoderm) cells, not the hypoblast.","Somites need mesoderm, which is not yet present."],"k":"Streak + node, no mesoderm yet = day ~16.","rn":[["","When does lateral folding start?","When does lateral folding start?","Day 20 onwards"]]},{"id":4085,"t":"Part 2 - Week 3: Gastrulation","s":"A researcher labels the epiblast in an early embryo. In the trilaminar disc, which layers would contain labelled cells?","o":["Ectoderm, mesoderm and most of the embryonic endoderm","Ectoderm and mesoderm, but no endoderm","Ectoderm only","Ectoderm, mesoderm and the umbilical vesicle endoderm","Mesoderm and endoderm, but not ectoderm"],"e":"The <b>epiblast</b> forms the <b>ectoderm</b>, and migrating ectoderm cells form the <b>embryonic mesoderm and endoderm</b>. The hypoblast forms only the umbilical vesicle endoderm.","w":["Most embryonic endoderm is from migrating epiblast.","Migrating epiblast cells form mesoderm and endoderm too.","Umbilical vesicle endoderm is from hypoblast.","The epiblast forms the ectoderm."],"k":"Epiblast → all three embryonic germ layers.","rn":[["","What are the 3 layers of the trilaminar disc?","What are the 3 layers of the trilaminar disc?","Ectoderm; Mesoderm; Endoderm"],["","Where do migrating ectoderm cells go?","Where do migrating ectoderm cells go?","Between ectoderm and hypoblast; Form mesoderm and endoderm"],["","Where does most embryonic endoderm come from?","Where does most embryonic endoderm come from?","Migrating ectoderm cells"]]},{"id":4086,"t":"Germ Layers and Their Derivatives","s":"A cell lineage study shows that cells of a structure came from the somites. Which structure could this be?","o":["Vertebrae","Epidermis","Liver parenchyma","Visceral pleura","Bladder mucosa"],"e":"Somites (paraxial mesoderm) divide into dermatomyotomes and <b>sclerotomes</b>; the sclerotome forms bone/skeleton such as <b>vertebrae</b>.","w":["Ectoderm.","Endoderm.","Lateral plate.","Endoderm."],"k":"Somites → sclerotome → vertebrae.","rn":[["","What does the Sclerotome form?","What does the Sclerotome form?","Vertebrae and skeleton (bone)"]]},{"id":4087,"t":"Periods of Development","s":"Which statement about the trophoblast and placenta is correct?","o":["Trophoblast forms the foetal placenta, which starts in the embryonic period","Trophoblast forms the foetal placenta, which starts in the foetal period","Embryoblast forms the foetal placenta, which starts in the embryonic period","Trophoblast forms the amnion, which starts in the embryonic period","Hypoblast forms the foetal placenta, which starts in the foetal period"],"e":"The <b>trophoblast</b> becomes the <b>foetal component of the placenta</b>; the <b>placenta starts to develop in the embryonic period</b> (weeks 3–8).","w":["It starts in the embryonic period (weeks 3–8).","The embryoblast forms the embryo.","Not the trophoblast fate.","The trophoblast forms it."],"k":"Trophoblast → placenta (starts in embryonic period).","rn":[["","What happens in the embryonic period?","What happens in the embryonic period?","Germ layers develop; Placenta starts to develop; Body systems form from the trilaminar disc"]]},{"id":4088,"t":"Week 1 of Development​","s":"Which cavity forms first?","o":["Blastocoele","Amniotic cavity","Primary yolk sac","Chorionic cavity","Peritoneal cavity"],"e":"The <b>blastocoele</b> forms at days 4–5. Then the amniotic cavity and primary yolk sac (days 8–10), the chorionic cavity (days 12–13), and the peritoneal cavity with folding (week 4).","w":["Days 8–10.","Days 8–10.","Days 12–13.","Week 4."],"k":"Cavities in order: blastocoele → amniotic/yolk → chorionic → peritoneal.","rn":[["","How does the blastocoele form?","How does the blastocoele form?","Cavities develop in the morula; Cavities merge"]]},{"id":4089,"t":"Part 2 - Week 2: Implantation and the Bilaminar Disc","s":"Which statement about the bilaminar and trilaminar discs is correct?","o":["Bilaminar from the embryoblast; trilaminar from epiblast and hypoblast","Bilaminar from the trophoblast; trilaminar from epiblast and hypoblast","Bilaminar from the embryoblast; trilaminar from the trophoblast","Bilaminar from the epiblast; trilaminar from the embryoblast","Bilaminar from the hypoblast; trilaminar from the blastocoele"],"e":"Summary: the <b>bilaminar disc</b> = derivatives of the <b>embryoblast</b> (epiblast, hypoblast). The <b>trilaminar disc</b> = derivatives of the <b>epiblast and hypoblast</b> (ectoderm, mesoderm from migrating ectoderm, endoderm).","w":["The trophoblast forms the placenta.","The trilaminar disc is from epiblast/hypoblast.","Order reversed.","Not correct."],"k":"Embryoblast → bilaminar → trilaminar.","rn":[["","What are the 2 layers of the bilaminar disc?","What are the 2 layers of the bilaminar disc?","Epiblast; Hypoblast"]]},{"id":4090,"t":"Part 2 - Week 3: Gastrulation","s":"A structure is formed by cells that migrate cranially from the primitive node, sits between the neural tube and aorta, and releases signals for cell migration. What is it?","o":["Notochord","Primitive streak","Neural crest","Somite","Connecting stalk"],"e":"The <b>notochord</b>: cells migrate cranially from the primitive node (days 17–21), it forms the longitudinal axis, lies between neural tube and aorta, and releases soluble mediators.","w":["Caudal midline depression.","Pinches off the neural tube.","Paraxial mesoderm blocks.","Becomes umbilical cord."],"k":"Notochord features.","rn":[["","Where do notochord cells come from?","Where do notochord cells come from?","Cranial migration from primitive node"],["","When does the notochord form?","When does the notochord form?","Days 17-21"],["","What does the notochord stimulate?","What does the notochord stimulate?","Migration of cells; Neural groove formation; Neural tube development"]]},{"id":4091,"t":"Week 1 of Development​","s":"Which statement about cleavage is <b>false</b>?","o":["Each division makes it larger","It involves mitosis","It produces blastomeres","It occurs in week 1","It produces identical daughter cells"],"e":"During cleavage the <b>overall size stays about the same</b>; the cells just get smaller.","w":["True.","True.","True.","True."],"k":"Cleavage: more, smaller cells; same overall size.","rn":[["","Cleavage","Cleavage","Repeated mitotic divisions of the zygote; Makes cells called blastomeres"]]},{"id":4092,"t":"Week 1 of Development​","s":"A colleague says the morula has a blastocoele. Why is this wrong?","o":["A blastocoele forms when cavities merge (blastocyst)","The blastocoele forms only after implantation in week 2","The blastocoele forms only in the bilaminar disc","The morula is a single cell with no room for a cavity","The morula has a blastocoele only after day 6"],"e":"The morula is a <b>solid</b> ball of 12–16 cells with no cavity. When cavities appear and merge into the <b>blastocoele</b>, the structure is a <b>blastocyst</b>.","w":["It forms days 4–5.","It is a blastocyst feature.","The morula is 12–16 cells.","A morula with a cavity is a blastocyst."],"k":"Morula = solid; blastocyst = has blastocoele.","rn":[["","How does the blastocoele form?","How does the blastocoele form?","Cavities develop in the morula; Cavities merge"]]},{"id":4093,"t":"Part 2 - Week 2: Implantation and the Bilaminar Disc","s":"Which pair of structures forms from the trophoblast during implantation?","o":["Syncytio- and cytotrophoblast","Epiblast and hypoblast","Amnion and exocoelomic membrane","Embryoblast and blastocoele","Syncytiotrophoblast and epiblast"],"e":"The <b>trophoblast differentiates</b> into the <b>syncytiotrophoblast</b> (maternal side) and <b>cytotrophoblast</b> (embryo side).","w":["From the embryoblast.","Not from the trophoblast.","Parts of the blastocyst.","The epiblast is embryoblast."],"k":"Trophoblast → syncytio- + cytotrophoblast.","rn":[["","What does the trophoblast differentiate into?","What does the trophoblast differentiate into?","Syncytiotrophoblast; Cytotrophoblast"],["","Cytotrophoblast","Cytotrophoblast","Embryo side; Single-cell layer; Left over from the blastocyst trophoblast"]]},{"id":4094,"t":"Germ Layers and Their Derivatives","s":"Which of these germ-layer derivative pairs would be affected together if the intermediate mesoderm failed to develop?","o":["Kidneys and reproductive organs","Skeleton and skeletal muscle","Epidermis and nervous system","Liver and pancreas parenchyma","Visceral and parietal pleura"],"e":"<b>Intermediate mesoderm</b> forms the <b>urogenital system</b>: urinary (kidneys, ureters, bladder, urethra) and genital (reproductive) organs.","w":["Paraxial.","Ectoderm.","Endoderm.","Lateral plate."],"k":"Intermediate mesoderm → urinary + reproductive.","rn":[["","What does urogenital mean?","What does urogenital mean?","Urinary: kidneys, ureters, bladder, urethra; Genital: male and female reproductive system"]]},{"id":4095,"t":"Germ Layers and Their Derivatives","s":"In the cross-section at 21 days, list the structures from the midline outwards.","o":["Notochord/neural tube → paraxial → intermediate → lateral plate","Notochord/neural tube → intermediate → paraxial → lateral plate","Notochord/neural tube → lateral plate → intermediate → paraxial","Lateral plate → intermediate → paraxial → notochord/neural tube","Notochord/neural tube → paraxial → lateral plate → intermediate"],"e":"Working outwards from the midline: <b>notochord and neural tube</b> → <b>paraxial</b> → <b>intermediate</b> → <b>lateral plate</b> (somatic and splanchnic layers).","w":["Paraxial is next to the axis.","Reversed.","This is lateral to medial.","Intermediate is in the middle."],"k":"Midline → paraxial → intermediate → lateral plate.","rn":[["","Paraxial Mesoderm","Paraxial Mesoderm","Immediately next to the axis; Flanks the notochord / neural tube; Increase in number of cells lateral to the neural tube"],["","Lateral Plate Mesoderm","Lateral Plate Mesoderm","Most lateral cell group from the neural tube"]]},{"id":4096,"t":"Part 2 - Week 2: Implantation and the Bilaminar Disc","s":"Which statement about the yolk sac is correct?","o":["It forms from the hypoblast cavity and becomes the secondary yolk sac","It forms from the epiblast cavity and becomes the secondary yolk sac","It forms from the hypoblast cavity and becomes the amniotic cavity","It forms from the blastocoele and becomes the chorionic cavity","It forms from the epiblast cavity and becomes the amniotic cavity"],"e":"<b>Hypoblast cavity = primary yolk sac</b> (nutrition) → <b>secondary yolk sac</b> (umbilical vesicle) after the chorionic cavity forms.","w":["The epiblast side forms the amniotic cavity.","The amniotic cavity is on the epiblast side.","The chorionic cavity forms from merged clefts.","That is the amniotic cavity, not the yolk sac."],"k":"Hypoblast → primary → secondary yolk sac.","rn":[["","Hypoblast","Hypoblast","Lower layer of the bilaminar disc; Forms the exocoelomic membrane; Its cavity is the primary yolk sac"],["","When does the primary yolk sac become the secondary yolk sac?","When does the primary yolk sac become the secondary yolk sac?","When the extra-embryonic coelom clefts unite; They form the chorionic cavity"],["","Secondary Yolk Sac","Secondary Yolk Sac","Umbilical Vesicle"]]},{"id":4097,"t":"Part 2 - Week 3: Gastrulation","s":"Which cells pinch off from the top of the closing neural tube?","o":["Neural crest","Somites","Notochord","Primitive node","Sclerotome"],"e":"The neurulation figure shows <b>neural crest</b> cells pinched off at the top of the neural tube, with <b>somites</b> lined up either side.","w":["Paraxial mesoderm.","Midline axis.","Primitive streak end.","Somite part."],"k":"Neural crest pinches off the neural tube.","rn":[["","When do brain vesicles form?","When do brain vesicles form?","Weeks 3-5; From the neural tube"],["","What does the notochord stimulate?","What does the notochord stimulate?","Migration of cells; Neural groove formation; Neural tube development"],["","What are the 3 stages of neurulation?","What are the 3 stages of neurulation?","Neural plate (day 18); Neural groove with neural folds (day 20); Neural tube (day 22)"]]},{"id":4098,"t":"Part 2 - Week 2: Implantation and the Bilaminar Disc","s":"Which statement about the amnion and amniotic cavity is correct?","o":["The cavity holds amniotic fluid around the embryo; the amnion lines it","The cavity holds amniotic fluid around the embryo; the hypoblast lines it","The cavity holds the yolk around the embryo; the amnion lines it","The amnion lines the yolk sac and becomes the umbilical cord","The cavity forms from the hypoblast on day 3"],"e":"The <b>amniotic cavity</b> contains the <b>amniotic fluid</b> surrounding the embryo; the <b>amnion</b> is its lining membrane (epiblast side).","w":["The hypoblast lines the yolk sac.","It holds amniotic fluid.","Not correct.","Not correct."],"k":"Amniotic cavity + amnion (epiblast side).","rn":[["","What does the amniotic cavity contain?","What does the amniotic cavity contain?","Amniotic fluid; Surrounds the developing embryo"],["","Epiblast","Epiblast","Upper layer of the bilaminar disc; Forms the amnion (maternal side); Its cavity is the amniotic cavity"],["","What is the amnion?","What is the amnion?","Membrane lining the amniotic cavity"]]},{"id":4099,"t":"Germ Layers and Their Derivatives","s":"Name the layer labelled <b>E</b>, which wraps around the gut tube.","e":"E is the splanchnic (visceral) layer of lateral plate mesoderm. It wraps the gut and forms visceral serous membranes (e.g. visceral pleura), smooth muscle and stroma of the organs; the gut lining itself comes from endoderm.","k":"E = splanchnic (visceral) mesoderm.","img":{"src":"4/mesoderm21","cap":"Transverse section of an embryo at 21 days. Labels removed."},"a":["splanchnic mesoderm","splanchnic","visceral mesoderm","splanchnic layer","visceral layer","splanchnic lateral plate mesoderm"],"rn":[["","What does the splanchnic (visceral) layer form?","What does the splanchnic (visceral) layer form?","Visceral membrane; Covers the organs"]]},{"id":4100,"t":"Germ Layers and Their Derivatives","s":"Which combination correctly links a structure with the germ layer and subdivision it comes from?","o":["Smooth muscle – mesoderm, lateral plate","Skeletal muscle – mesoderm, lateral plate","Dermis – ectoderm","Kidney – mesoderm, paraxial","Parathyroid parenchyma – mesoderm, intermediate"],"e":"<b>Smooth muscle</b> comes from <b>lateral plate mesoderm</b>. Skeletal muscle and dermis are paraxial; kidneys intermediate; parathyroid parenchyma endoderm.","w":["Paraxial.","Paraxial mesoderm.","Intermediate.","Endoderm."],"k":"Smooth muscle: lateral plate.","rn":[["","What does Lateral Plate Mesoderm form?","What does Lateral Plate Mesoderm form?","Serous membranes; Smooth muscle; Stroma"],["","Which germ layers make smooth muscle, stroma and the inner lining of viscera?","Which germ layers make smooth muscle, stroma and the inner lining of viscera?","Lateral plate mesoderm: smooth muscle, stroma; Endoderm: epithelium (inner lining)"]]},{"id":4101,"t":"Part 2 - Week 2: Implantation and the Bilaminar Disc","s":"Which statement about the two trophoblast layers is correct?","o":["The cytotrophoblast is the single-cell layer on the embryo side","The cytotrophoblast is the layer that keeps invading the endometrium","The syncytiotrophoblast is the single-cell layer on the embryo side","Both layers come from the embryoblast during implantation","The syncytiotrophoblast forms the bilaminar disc"],"e":"The <b>syncytiotrophoblast</b> keeps invading (maternal side); the <b>cytotrophoblast</b> is the single-cell layer left on the <b>embryo side</b>.","w":["That is the syncytiotrophoblast.","It is on the maternal side.","They come from the trophoblast.","The embryoblast forms the disc."],"k":"Cytotrophoblast = embryo side.","rn":[["","Cytotrophoblast","Cytotrophoblast","Embryo side; Single-cell layer; Left over from the blastocyst trophoblast"]]},{"id":4102,"t":"Part 2 - Week 3: Gastrulation","s":"Which statement about directional terms during development is correct?","o":["The streak forms at the caudal end and the node at the cephalic end","The streak forms at the cephalic end and the node at the caudal end","The streak and node both form at the caudal end","The streak and node both form at the cephalic end","The streak forms in the midline and the node at the lateral edge"],"e":"Streak: <b>caudal</b> end (tail). Node: <b>cephalic</b> end of the streak (head). Caudal = tail; cephalic/cranial = head.","w":["Reversed.","The node is cephalic.","The streak is caudal.","The node is at the cephalic end of the streak."],"k":"Streak caudal; node cephalic.","rn":[["","Primitive Node","Primitive Node","At the cephalic (head) end of the primitive streak; Day 16"]]},{"id":4103,"t":"Week 4: Folding of the Embryo","s":"Name the part of the gut tube labelled <b>A</b>, at the cranial (head) end of the folded embryo.","e":"Longitudinal (cephalocaudal) folding, driven by enlargement of the brain vesicles, curls the head and tail ventrally and pinches the yolk sac into foregut (cranial), midgut and hindgut (caudal).","k":"A = foregut.","img":{"src":"4/folding25","cap":"Sagittal section of an embryo at day 25, during longitudinal folding. Labels removed."},"a":["foregut","fore gut"],"rn":[["","What does longitudinal folding form?","What does longitudinal folding form?","Foregut; Midgut; Hindgut"]]},{"id":4104,"t":"Week 4: Folding of the Embryo","s":"Name the duct labelled <b>D</b>, which keeps one part of the gut connected to the yolk sac after folding.","e":"During longitudinal folding the midgut stays connected to the yolk sac by the vitello-intestinal duct (D). The foregut and hindgut are pinched off at the head and tail ends.","k":"D = vitello-intestinal duct.","img":{"src":"4/folding25","cap":"Sagittal section of an embryo at day 25, during longitudinal folding. Labels removed."},"a":["vitello-intestinal duct","vitellointestinal duct","vitello intestinal duct","vitelline duct","omphalomesenteric duct"],"rn":[["","What joins the midgut to the yolk sac?","What joins the midgut to the yolk sac?","Vitello-intestinal duct"]]},{"id":4105,"t":"Germ Layers and Their Derivatives","s":"Which pair of structures share an origin from the splanchnic (visceral) layer of lateral plate mesoderm?","o":["Visceral pleura and the smooth muscle of the gut wall","Parietal pleura and the smooth muscle of the gut wall","Visceral pleura and the epithelium of the gut","Visceral pleura and the dermis of the skin","Parietal pleura and the dermis of the skin"],"e":"The splanchnic layer wraps the gut, forming <b>visceral membranes</b> (e.g. visceral pleura); lateral plate mesoderm also forms <b>smooth muscle</b>.","w":["Parietal is somatic layer.","Epithelium is endoderm.","Dermis is paraxial.","Neither is splanchnic."],"k":"Splanchnic layer: visceral membranes + smooth muscle.","rn":[["","What does Lateral Plate Mesoderm form?","What does Lateral Plate Mesoderm form?","Serous membranes; Smooth muscle; Stroma"],["","Which germ layers make smooth muscle, stroma and the inner lining of viscera?","Which germ layers make smooth muscle, stroma and the inner lining of viscera?","Lateral plate mesoderm: smooth muscle, stroma; Endoderm: epithelium (inner lining)"]]},{"id":4106,"t":"Week 1 of Development​","s":"A conceptus has a fully formed blastocoele, an outer single-cell layer and an inner cell mass, but no epiblast or hypoblast. What is its approximate age?","o":["Days 4–5","Day 1","Days 8–10","Day 15","Week 4"],"e":"A <b>blastocyst</b> (blastocoele, trophoblast, embryoblast) is fully formed at <b>days 4–5</b>; epiblast/hypoblast appear at days 8–10.","w":["Zygote.","Bilaminar disc.","Primitive streak.","Folding."],"k":"Blastocyst: days 4–5.","rn":[["","When is the blastocyst fully formed?","When is the blastocyst fully formed?","Days 4-5"]]},{"id":4107,"t":"Part 2 - Week 3: Gastrulation","s":"Which statement about mesoderm formation is correct?","o":["All of the mesoderm comes from migrating epiblast cells","All of the mesoderm comes from the hypoblast","Most of the mesoderm comes from the trophoblast","All of the mesoderm forms in week 1","Mesoderm comes from both epiblast and hypoblast equally"],"e":"<b>All of the mesoderm</b> (and most of the embryonic endoderm) comes from <b>migrating ectoderm (epiblast) cells</b>.","w":["The hypoblast forms umbilical vesicle endoderm.","The trophoblast forms the placenta.","Mesoderm forms in week 3.","All is from migrating epiblast."],"k":"Mesoderm ← migrating epiblast.","rn":[["","Where does most embryonic endoderm come from?","Where does most embryonic endoderm come from?","Migrating ectoderm cells"]]},{"id":4108,"t":"Week 4: Folding of the Embryo","s":"When the lateral body walls meet ventrally during folding, which body cavity do they close off?","e":"Lateral folding (driven by somites) brings the sides together ventrally, closing the future peritoneal (abdominal) cavity, lined by serous membranes from lateral plate mesoderm.","k":"Peritoneal cavity.","a":["peritoneal cavity","peritoneal","abdominal cavity","peritoneum"],"rn":[["","Which cavity does lateral folding close?","Which cavity does lateral folding close?","Abdominal / peritoneal cavity"]]},{"id":4109,"t":"Week 1 of Development​","s":"Which statement about fertilisation is <b>false</b>?","o":["Several spermatozoa fertilise the oocyte together","Only one spermatozoon fertilises the oocyte","Fertilisation normally occurs in the uterine tube","Fertilisation produces a diploid zygote","Fertilisation occurs 12–24 hours after ovulation"],"e":"Many spermatozoa surround the oocyte, but <b>only one</b> fertilises it.","w":["True.","True.","True.","True."],"k":"Only one spermatozoon fertilises.","rn":[["","How many spermatozoa fertilise the oocyte?","How many spermatozoa fertilise the oocyte?","One"]]},{"id":4110,"t":"Week 4: Folding of the Embryo","s":"Name the part of the gut labelled <b>C</b>, formed as the tail end curls ventrally.","e":"The tail fold curls under and pinches off the caudal part of the yolk sac as the hindgut (C).","k":"C = hindgut.","img":{"src":"4/folding25","cap":"Sagittal section of an embryo at day 25, during longitudinal folding. Labels removed."},"a":["hindgut","hind gut"],"rn":[["","What does longitudinal folding form?","What does longitudinal folding form?","Foregut; Midgut; Hindgut"]]},{"id":4111,"t":"Part 2 - Week 2: Implantation and the Bilaminar Disc","s":"Which of the following best describes the hypoblast cavity's lining?","o":["Exocoelomic membrane","Amnion","Syncytiotrophoblast","Zona pellucida","Parietal pleura"],"e":"<b>Hypoblast = exocoelomic membrane</b>; hypoblast cavity = primary yolk sac.","w":["Epiblast cavity.","Trophoblast.","Oocyte barrier.","Adult membrane."],"k":"Primary yolk sac lined by exocoelomic membrane.","rn":[["","Hypoblast","Hypoblast","Lower layer of the bilaminar disc; Forms the exocoelomic membrane; Its cavity is the primary yolk sac"]]},{"id":4112,"t":"Germ Layers and Their Derivatives","s":"Which pair of structures are formed from the dermatomyotome?","o":["Dermis and skeletal muscle","Epidermis and nerves","Vertebrae and ribs","Serous membranes and smooth muscle","Kidneys and gonads"],"e":"The <b>dermatomyotome</b> = <b>dermatome</b> (dermis) + <b>myotome</b> (skeletal muscle). The sclerotome forms bone.","w":["Ectoderm.","Sclerotome (bone).","Lateral plate.","Intermediate."],"k":"Dermatomyotome → dermis + muscle.","rn":[["","What does Paraxial Mesoderm form?","What does Paraxial Mesoderm form?","Skeletal muscle; Majority of the skeleton; Dermis; Connective tissue"],["","What does the Myotome form?","What does the Myotome form?","Skeletal muscle"]]},{"id":4113,"t":"Week 4: Folding of the Embryo","s":"Which statement about the part labelled <b>B</b> is correct?","o":["It is the midgut, connected to E by D","It is the foregut, connected to E by D","It is the midgut, connected to E by the connecting stalk","It is the hindgut, pinched off completely from E","It is the midgut, lined by mesoderm"],"e":"B is the midgut. After longitudinal folding the midgut stays connected to the yolk sac (E) by the vitello-intestinal duct (D). The secondary yolk sac is incorporated into the umbilical cord and helps form the GI tract; the gut epithelium is endodermal.","w":["The foregut is at the head end (A).","The connecting stalk becomes the umbilical cord; the midgut joins the yolk sac by the vitello-intestinal duct.","B is in the middle, not at the tail end.","The gut lining (epithelium) comes from endoderm."],"k":"Midgut ↔ yolk sac via vitello-intestinal duct.","img":{"src":"4/folding25","cap":"Sagittal section of an embryo at day 25, during longitudinal folding. Labels removed."},"rn":[["","What joins the midgut to the yolk sac?","What joins the midgut to the yolk sac?","Vitello-intestinal duct"]]},{"id":4114,"t":"Week 1 of Development​","s":"Which statement about the 16-cell stage is correct?","o":["It is the morula, a “16 cell stage mass”","It is the blastocyst, a “16 cell stage mass”","It is the morula, formed by meiosis","It is the blastocyst, formed in week 2","It is the morula, formed in week 2"],"e":"The summary describes the <b>morula</b> simply as a “<b>16 cell stage mass</b>” (12–16 cells), around day 3.","w":["The blastocyst has a cavity.","Cleavage is by mitosis.","The blastocyst forms days 4–5.","The morula forms around day 3."],"k":"Morula = 16-cell stage mass.","rn":[["","Morula","Morula","16 cell stage embryo; Solid ball of blastomeres"],["","Morula: how many cells does it have?","Morula: how many cells does it have?","12-16 cells"]]},{"id":4115,"t":"Germ Layers and Their Derivatives","s":"A congenital abnormality affects the epithelium and glands of the respiratory tract. Name the germ layer involved.","e":"Endoderm forms the epithelium of the gut and respiratory tract and its glands, the parenchyma of liver, pancreas, thyroid and parathyroids, bladder mucosa and lung tissue.","k":"Endoderm.","a":["endoderm","endodermal"],"rn":[["","What does Endoderm form?","What does Endoderm form?","Epithelium of the alimentary tract; Epithelium and glands of the respiratory tract; Parenchymal cells (liver, pancreas, thyroid, parathyroid); Mucosa of the bladder; Lung tissue (parenchyma)"]]},{"id":4116,"t":"Periods of Development","s":"Which change marks the transition from the embryonic to the foetal period?","o":["The end of week 8, after which the conceptus mainly grows and matures","The end of week 2, after which the conceptus mainly grows and matures","The end of week 4, after folding has finished","The end of week 3, after the trilaminar disc forms","The end of week 6, after the neural tube closes"],"e":"The embryonic period ends at <b>week 8</b>; from <b>week 9</b> the foetus mainly grows and matures.","w":["Week 2 ends the pre-embryonic period.","Folding is week 4, still embryonic.","Gastrulation is week 3.","Embryonic period ends at week 8."],"k":"Week 8/9 boundary: embryo → foetus.","rn":[["","When is the embryonic period?","When is the embryonic period?","Week 3 to end of week 8"]]},{"id":4117,"t":"Week 1 of Development​","s":"Which structure sits inside the zona pellucida at day 3 but has no cavity, while the structure at day 5 has a cavity?","o":["Morula (day 3), blastocyst (day 5)","Zygote (day 3), morula (day 5)","Blastocyst (day 3), bilaminar disc (day 5)","Morula (day 3), bilaminar disc (day 5)","Ovum (day 3), zygote (day 5)"],"e":"Day 3: <b>morula</b> (solid). Days 4–5: <b>blastocyst</b> (blastocoele).","w":["Zygote is day 0.","Reversed timing.","Bilaminar disc is days 8–10.","Day 0."],"k":"Day 3 morula → day 4–5 blastocyst.","rn":[["","When does the morula form?","When does the morula form?","Around day 3"]]},{"id":4118,"t":"Part 2 - Week 3: Gastrulation","s":"Which of the following is the correct pairing of structure and the process that forms it?","o":["Neural tube – neurulation","Trilaminar disc – cleavage","Blastomeres – gastrulation","Chorionic cavity – neurulation","Foregut – implantation"],"e":"<b>Neurulation</b> forms the <b>neural tube</b>. Gastrulation forms the trilaminar disc; cleavage forms blastomeres; the chorionic cavity forms from merged coelom; the foregut from longitudinal folding.","w":["Gastrulation.","Cleavage.","Merged coelom clefts.","Folding."],"k":"Match process to product.","rn":[["","Neurulation","Neurulation","Formation of the neural tube; Days 18-25"],["","What does neurulation form from the neural tube?","What does neurulation form from the neural tube?","Brain and ventricles; Spinal cord (rest of the tube)"],["","What are the 3 stages of neurulation?","What are the 3 stages of neurulation?","Neural plate (day 18); Neural groove with neural folds (day 20); Neural tube (day 22)"]]},{"id":4119,"t":"Part 2 - Week 2: Implantation and the Bilaminar Disc","s":"Which statement about the secondary yolk sac and the gut is correct?","o":["It joins the umbilical cord and connects with the midgut when the embryo folds","It joins the umbilical cord and connects with the foregut when the embryo folds","It becomes the amniotic cavity when the embryo folds","It disappears before implantation is complete","It becomes the hindgut directly when the embryo folds"],"e":"The umbilical vesicle (secondary yolk sac) is incorporated into the umbilical cord, <b>connecting with the midgut</b> when the embryo folds (via the vitello-intestinal duct).","w":["It connects with the midgut.","Not described.","It persists in the cord.","It connects with the midgut."],"k":"Yolk sac connects with midgut.","rn":[["","Connecting Stalk","Connecting Stalk","Develops into the umbilical cord; The embryo hangs from it in the chorionic cavity"],["","What joins the midgut to the yolk sac?","What joins the midgut to the yolk sac?","Vitello-intestinal duct"]]},{"id":4120,"t":"Part 2 - Week 2: Implantation and the Bilaminar Disc","s":"The structure labelled <b>E</b> originally formed as a cavity of which layer, and what did it provide?","o":["Hypoblast; nutrition","Epiblast; nutrition","Hypoblast; amniotic fluid","Trophoblast; placental blood flow","Epiblast; amniotic fluid"],"e":"E is the yolk sac. The primary yolk sac forms on the hypoblast side of the bilaminar disc, lined by the exocoelomic membrane, and nourishes the embryo; it becomes the secondary yolk sac when the chorionic cavity forms.","w":["The epiblast forms the amniotic cavity.","Amniotic fluid fills the amniotic cavity (epiblast side).","The trophoblast forms the foetal placenta.","That is the amniotic cavity."],"k":"Yolk sac: hypoblast side, nutrition.","img":{"src":"4/folding25","cap":"Sagittal section of an embryo at day 25, during longitudinal folding. Labels removed."},"rn":[["","Hypoblast","Hypoblast","Lower layer of the bilaminar disc; Forms the exocoelomic membrane; Its cavity is the primary yolk sac"]]},{"id":4121,"t":"Week 1 of Development​","s":"A 29-year-old woman is tracking her cycle and knows she ovulated on a Monday morning. She has intercourse that evening. In a teaching example, her tutor follows what happens next in the uterine tube: the spermatozoon and oocyte unite, two pronuclei form and then join.<br><br>When does fertilisation most likely occur, and what is the resulting cell?","o":["Within 12–24 hours of ovulation; a diploid zygote with 46 chromosomes","About 3 days after ovulation; a 12–16 cell morula","Within 12–24 hours of ovulation; a haploid blastomere","About 4–5 days after ovulation; a blastocyst with a blastocoele cavity and an inner cell mass","On days 6–13; an implanted syncytiotrophoblast"],"e":"Step 1: fertilisation happens 12–24 hours after ovulation, when a spermatozoon and an oocyte unite in the reproductive tract. Step 2: a female and a male pronucleus form and join to make a zygote, a diploid cell with 46 chromosomes, which then undergoes cleavage.","w":["The morula forms about 3 days after fertilisation, not at fertilisation.","The zygote is diploid; blastomeres are the cells formed later by cleavage.","The blastocyst forms at days 4–5, after the morula.","Days 6–13 is implantation, and the syncytiotrophoblast comes from the trophoblast."],"k":"Fertilisation 12–24 h after ovulation → diploid zygote (46).","rn":[["","Zygote","Zygote","Formed when the 2 pronuclei join; Diploid: 46 chromosomes"],["","When does fertilisation occur?","When does fertilisation occur?","12-24 hours after ovulation"]]},{"id":4122,"t":"Week 1 of Development​","s":"In an IVF (in vitro fertilisation) laboratory, an embryologist examines an embryo on day 5. It has a large fluid-filled cavity, a cluster of cells at one side, and a thin single layer of outer cells. She chooses a sample of cells for testing that will not form part of the baby itself, but will help it attach to the uterus.<br><br>Which part does she sample, and what will it become?","o":["Trophoblast; it invades the uterus and forms the foetal part of the placenta","Embryoblast; it becomes the embryo via the bilaminar disc","Blastocoele; it becomes the amniotic cavity, which holds the fluid that surrounds the embryo","Hypoblast; it forms the ectoderm of the embryo","Epiblast; it invades the uterine epithelium"],"e":"Step 1: a day-5 embryo with a fluid-filled cavity (blastocoele), an inner cell mass (embryoblast) and an outer single-cell layer (trophoblast) is a blastocyst. Step 2: the trophoblast is the part that does not form the embryo; it invades the uterus and becomes the foetal component of the placenta, while the embryoblast becomes the embryo.","w":["The embryoblast becomes the embryo itself, which she wants to avoid.","The blastocoele is a fluid cavity, not cells to sample.","The hypoblast is the lower bilaminar layer and does not form ectoderm.","The epiblast is part of the bilaminar disc that forms later from the embryoblast."],"k":"Trophoblast → placenta; embryoblast → embryo.","rn":[["","Trophoblast","Trophoblast","Outer cell layer of the blastocyst; A single layer of cells; Becomes the foetal part of the placenta"]]},{"id":4123,"t":"Part 2 - Week 2: Implantation and the Bilaminar Disc","s":"An early pregnancy is studied at about day 9 after fertilisation. The blastocyst has burrowed into the uterine epithelium and endometrium. The outer layer has split into two parts: one in contact with maternal tissue, and one on the side of the embryo. The inner cell mass has formed two layers.<br><br>Which pairing of layers is correct?","o":["Syncytiotrophoblast on the maternal side; epiblast and hypoblast in the disc","Cytotrophoblast on the maternal side; ectoderm and endoderm in the disc","Syncytiotrophoblast on the embryo side; ectoderm, mesoderm and endoderm in the disc","Cytotrophoblast on the maternal side; notochord and neural tube in the disc, as gastrulation has finished","Epiblast on the maternal side; trophoblast and amnion in the disc"],"e":"Step 1: during implantation (days 6–13), the trophoblast differentiates into the syncytiotrophoblast on the maternal side and the cytotrophoblast on the embryo side. Step 2: at days 8–10 the inner cell mass forms the bilaminar disc of epiblast (upper) and hypoblast (lower); three germ layers do not appear until gastrulation in week 3.","w":["The cytotrophoblast is on the embryo side, and ectoderm/endoderm appear in week 3.","The syncytiotrophoblast is on the maternal side, and the disc has only two layers now.","The notochord and neural tube form in week 3, not by day 9.","The epiblast is part of the disc, not the outer trophoblast layer."],"k":"Syncytio = maternal side; bilaminar disc = epiblast + hypoblast (days 8–10).","rn":[["","Epiblast","Epiblast","Upper layer of the bilaminar disc; Forms the amnion (maternal side); Its cavity is the amniotic cavity"],["","What are the 2 layers of the bilaminar disc?","What are the 2 layers of the bilaminar disc?","Epiblast; Hypoblast"]]},{"id":4124,"t":"Part 2 - Week 3: Gastrulation","s":"A researcher labels the cells of the upper layer of a bilaminar disc with a fluorescent dye on day 14. By day 20, she finds the dye in the outermost layer, but also in most of the inner layer and all of the middle layer of the new three-layered disc. Only the endoderm of the umbilical vesicle is unlabelled.<br><br>Which statement explains her results?","o":["Epiblast cells migrate through the primitive streak to form mesoderm and endoderm","Hypoblast cells form all the mesoderm and most of the embryonic endoderm","The trophoblast forms the mesoderm, so the dye should not appear there","The primitive node forms the ectoderm, and the hypoblast forms the whole mesoderm layer of the new disc","Neurulation forms all three germ layers from the neural tube"],"e":"Step 1: the upper layer of the bilaminar disc is the epiblast, which forms the ectoderm (outermost layer) during gastrulation in week 3. Step 2: epiblast (ectoderm) cells also migrate through the primitive streak to form the embryonic mesoderm and endoderm, while the hypoblast forms only the endoderm of the umbilical vesicle (secondary yolk sac), so only that is unlabelled.","w":["Most endoderm and all mesoderm come from migrating epiblast cells, not the hypoblast.","The trophoblast forms placenta, not germ layers.","The primitive node gives rise to the notochord, and the hypoblast does not form mesoderm.","Neurulation forms the neural tube after gastrulation."],"k":"Gastrulation: epiblast → ectoderm + (by migration) mesoderm and endoderm.","rn":[["","What makes ectoderm cells migrate?","What makes ectoderm cells migrate?","Formation of the primitive streak"]]},{"id":4125,"t":"Part 2 - Week 3: Gastrulation","s":"An embryo is examined on day 16. A midline depression is seen at one end of the disc, with a small knot of cells at its other end. Over the next few days, cells move from this knot towards the head end and form a rod along the midline of the embryo.<br><br>Which structures are these, and what does the rod do?","o":["Primitive streak (caudal) and node (cephalic); the rod is the notochord, the longitudinal axis","Neural groove and neural folds; the rod is the neural tube","Primitive node (caudal) and streak (cephalic); the rod is a somite","Connecting stalk and chorionic cavity; the rod is the umbilical cord","Neural plate and neural crest; the rod is the spinal cord running the whole length of the embryo's back"],"e":"Step 1: the primitive streak (day 15) is a midline depression at the caudal end of the bilaminar disc, and the primitive node (day 16) lies at its cephalic (head) end. Step 2: cells migrate cranially from the primitive node to form the notochordal plate and then the notochord (days 17–21), which forms the longitudinal axis of the embryo.","w":["Neural groove and folds come from the neural plate during neurulation.","The streak is caudal and the node cephalic, and somites are mesoderm blocks.","The connecting stalk becomes the umbilical cord; it is not a midline rod.","Neural crest is not in this lecture, and the neural tube is a separate later structure."],"k":"Streak caudal, node cephalic; node → notochord (body axis).","rn":[["","Primitive Node","Primitive Node","At the cephalic (head) end of the primitive streak; Day 16"],["","Primitive Streak","Primitive Streak","Midline depression; Caudal end of the bilaminar disc; Day 15"]]},{"id":4126,"t":"Part 2 - Week 3: Gastrulation","s":"A baby is born with a problem in the closing of the structure that forms the central nervous system. In a teaching session, students trace normal development: a flat plate of tissue forms a groove with folds, which closes into a tube. The head end of the tube then swells into three primary vesicles.<br><br>What is this process called, and which secondary vesicles come from the forebrain vesicle?","o":["Neurulation; telencephalon and diencephalon","Gastrulation; metencephalon and myelencephalon","Neurulation; mesencephalon only","Folding; telencephalon and metencephalon","Cleavage; diencephalon and myelencephalon, from the hindbrain"],"e":"Step 1: neurulation (days 18–25) is the formation of the neural tube: neural plate → neural groove with neural folds → neural tube. Step 2: the brain forms as 3 primary vesicles that divide into 5 secondary vesicles; the prosencephalon (forebrain) gives the telencephalon and diencephalon, the mesencephalon stays as one, and the rhombencephalon gives the metencephalon and myelencephalon.","w":["Gastrulation forms the trilaminar disc, and those vesicles are from the hindbrain.","The mesencephalon is the midbrain vesicle.","Folding shapes the embryo; the metencephalon is a hindbrain vesicle.","Cleavage is mitotic division of the zygote."],"k":"Neurulation → neural tube; forebrain → telencephalon + diencephalon.","rn":[["","What are the 5 secondary brain vesicles?","What are the 5 secondary brain vesicles?","Telencephalon; Diencephalon; Mesencephalon; Metencephalon; Myelencephalon"],["","What does the prosencephalon divide into?","What does the prosencephalon divide into?","Telencephalon; Diencephalon"]]},{"id":4127,"t":"Germ Layers and Their Derivatives","s":"A child is born with abnormal kidneys and reproductive organs, while the skeleton, skeletal muscle and the linings of the body cavities are normal. A genetics team suspects a problem in one strip of the middle germ layer during week 3. They note the problem strip is slightly lateral to the strip next to the notochord.<br><br>Which part of the mesoderm is affected?","o":["Intermediate mesoderm, which forms the urogenital system","Paraxial mesoderm, which forms the skeleton, skeletal muscle, dermis and connective tissue","Lateral plate mesoderm, which forms serous membranes","Endoderm, which forms the bladder mucosa","Ectoderm, which forms the nervous system"],"e":"Step 1: on day 17 the mesoderm divides (medial to lateral) into paraxial, intermediate and lateral plate parts. Step 2: the intermediate mesoderm, slightly lateral to the paraxial mesoderm, forms the urogenital system, so kidney and reproductive defects with a normal skeleton and serous membranes point to it.","w":["Paraxial mesoderm forms the skeleton and muscle, which are normal here.","Lateral plate mesoderm forms serous membranes, which are normal.","Endoderm is not mesoderm, and kidney and reproductive organs are urogenital.","Ectoderm is the outer layer and is not mesoderm."],"k":"Intermediate mesoderm → urogenital system.","rn":[["","Intermediate Mesoderm","Intermediate Mesoderm","Slightly more lateral than paraxial mesoderm; Forms the urogenital system"]]},{"id":4128,"t":"Germ Layers and Their Derivatives","s":"A surgeon operating in the chest notes two membranes: one lining the inside of the chest wall and one closely covering the surface of the lung. A student asks where these two membranes came from in the embryo. The surgeon explains they come from two layers of the same part of the mesoderm.<br><br>Which pairing is correct?","o":["Somatic layer → parietal pleura; splanchnic layer → visceral pleura","Splanchnic layer → parietal pleura; somatic layer → visceral pleura","Paraxial mesoderm → parietal pleura; intermediate mesoderm → visceral pleura","Ectoderm → parietal pleura; endoderm → visceral pleura","Somatic layer → parietal pleura; the visceral pleura comes from endoderm, like the lung tissue itself"],"e":"Step 1: serous membranes come from the lateral plate mesoderm, which splits into a somatic (parietal) layer and a splanchnic (visceral) layer. Step 2: the somatic layer lines the body cavity wall (parietal pleura lining the pleural cavity), and the splanchnic layer covers the organs (visceral pleura surrounding the lung).","w":["The layers are reversed.","Paraxial forms skeleton and muscle; intermediate forms the urogenital system.","Serous membranes are mesoderm, not ecto- or endoderm.","The visceral pleura comes from the splanchnic mesoderm; only lung tissue is endoderm."],"k":"Somatic → parietal membrane; splanchnic → visceral membrane.","rn":[["","What are parietal and visceral pleura?","What are parietal and visceral pleura?","Parietal: lines the pleural (thoracic) cavity; Visceral: surrounds the lung"],["","What does the splanchnic (visceral) layer form?","What does the splanchnic (visceral) layer form?","Visceral membrane; Covers the organs"],["","What does the somatic (parietal) layer form?","What does the somatic (parietal) layer form?","Parietal membrane; Lines the body cavity wall"]]},{"id":4129,"t":"Germ Layers and Their Derivatives","s":"A 45-year-old patient has disease affecting several organs: the lining epithelium of the gut, the parenchymal cells of the pancreas and liver, and the mucosa of the bladder. A second patient has disease of the epidermis and of the eyes and inner ear. Both conditions are thought to start from a single germ layer.<br><br>Which germ layers are involved in the two patients?","o":["Patient 1: endoderm; patient 2: ectoderm","Patient 1: mesoderm; patient 2: endoderm","Patient 1: ectoderm; patient 2: mesoderm","Patient 1: endoderm; patient 2: mesoderm","Both mesoderm, the middle layer that forms the urogenital system and serous membranes"],"e":"Step 1: the endoderm forms the epithelium of the alimentary and respiratory tracts, the parenchyma of the liver, pancreas, thyroid and parathyroids, and the bladder mucosa (patient 1). Step 2: the ectoderm forms the epidermis, the viscerocranium and the nervous system including the eyes and inner ear (patient 2).","w":["Gut epithelium and liver parenchyma are endoderm, and skin/eyes are ectoderm.","The pairs are reversed and wrong.","Epidermis and eyes are ectoderm.","Mesoderm forms muscle, skeleton, urogenital system and serous membranes."],"k":"Endoderm: gut/respiratory epithelium, organ parenchyma; ectoderm: skin, nervous system.","rn":[["","What does Endoderm form?","What does Endoderm form?","Epithelium of the alimentary tract; Epithelium and glands of the respiratory tract; Parenchymal cells (liver, pancreas, thyroid, parathyroid); Mucosa of the bladder; Lung tissue (parenchyma)"],["","What does Ectoderm form?","What does Ectoderm form?","Epidermis; Viscerocranium; Nervous system (including eyes and inner ear)"]]},{"id":4130,"t":"Week 4: Folding of the Embryo","s":"In week 4, an embryologist watches a flat trilaminar disc change shape. The head end grows quickly as the brain vesicles enlarge, and the head and tail ends curl ventrally. At the same time, rows of 42–44 somite pairs develop, and the sides of the disc fold towards each other and meet.<br><br>What does each type of folding produce?","o":["Longitudinal folding forms the foregut, midgut and hindgut; lateral folding closes the body cavity","Lateral folding forms the foregut and hindgut; longitudinal folding closes the body cavity","Longitudinal folding forms the neural tube; lateral folding forms the notochord","Both foldings form the placenta from the trophoblast and the connecting stalk","Longitudinal folding forms the somites; lateral folding forms the primitive streak"],"e":"Step 1: longitudinal (cephalocaudal) folding on days 21–24 is driven by enlargement of the brain vesicles; the head and tail folds curl ventrally and form the foregut, midgut and hindgut. Step 2: lateral (transverse) folding from day 20 is driven by somite development; the sides fold ventrally and meet, closing the body cavity (the future peritoneal cavity).","w":["The two results are swapped.","The neural tube forms by neurulation and the notochord from the node.","The placenta comes from the trophoblast, not folding.","Somites drive lateral folding, and the streak forms on day 15."],"k":"Longitudinal fold → fore/mid/hindgut; lateral fold → closes body cavity.","rn":[["","What does longitudinal folding form?","What does longitudinal folding form?","Foregut; Midgut; Hindgut"],["","What does lateral folding do?","What does lateral folding do?","Sides fold ventrally and meet; Closes off the body cavity"]]}]);
